ese for an additional 3 minutes.\\n\\nAnswer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350°F (175°C), season the fillets with paprika and black pepper, place them on a sprayed baking sheet (preferably with vegetables), and bake for about 15 minutes until the fish is cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, add cheese and bake for an additional 3 minutes to melt.\", \"Step 1: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C) as mentioned in passage 2.\\nStep 2: Season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, as directed in both passage 1 and passage 2.\\nStep 3: Place the seasoned pollock fillets on a baking sheet. In passage 1, it is recommended to spray the baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray before placing the fillets.\\nStep 4: Add toppings such as onion, green pepper, and tomato slices as described in passage 1.\\nStep 5: Bake in the preheated oven for approximately 15 minutes until the fish is cooked and flakes easily, as mentioned in passages 1, 2, and 3.\\nStep 6: If desired, turn off the oven, place cheese slices on each fillet, and return to the oven for about 3 minutes to melt the cheese, as stated in passage 3.\\n\\nAnswer: Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C). Season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place on a greased baking sheet and optionally add onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake for 15 minutes until cooked and flakes easily. For a cheesy version, top with cheese after baking and return to oven for 3 minutes to melt.\"]", "time_sec": 206.101, "generation_time_seconds": 206.0979, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0002, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.62, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "38", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to cook pollock:\n\nPreheat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the fillets on a baking sheet lined with parchment paper or aluminum foil. You can also add sliced onions, green peppers, and tomatoes on top of the fillets. Bake for 15 minutes or until the fish flakes easily with a fork. If desired, top with cheese and return to the oven for an additional 3 minutes or until the cheese is melted.\n\nTherefore, the answer is not \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"", "pred": "Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C), season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on a baking sheet (optionally with vegetables), bake for about 15 minutes until cooked and flaky. Optionally, add cheese and return for 3 minutes to melt.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C) as mentioned in passage 2.\n\nStep 2: Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, as stated in both passage 1 and passage 2.\n\nStep 3: Place the seasoned pollock fillets on a baking sheet. If following passage 1, spray the baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray and layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices.\n\nStep 4: Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes until the fish is cooked and flakes easily. This is consistent across all three passages.\n\nStep 5: For a variation, after the fish is cooked, as mentioned in passage 3, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return to the oven for about 3 minutes to melt the cheese.\n\nFinal Answer: Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C), season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on a baking sheet (optionally with vegetables), bake for about 15 minutes until cooked and flaky. Optionally, add cheese and return for 3 minutes to melt.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C) as mentioned in passage 2.\\n\\nStep 2: Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, as stated in both passage 1 and passage 2.\\n\\nStep 3: Place the seasoned pollock fillets on a baking sheet. If following passage 1, spray the baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray and layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices.\\n\\nStep 4: Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes until the fish is cooked and flakes easily. This is consistent across all three passages.\\n\\nStep 5: For a variation, after the fish is cooked, as mentioned in passage 3, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return to the oven for about 3 minutes to melt the cheese.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C), season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on a baking sheet (optionally with vegetables), bake for about 15 minutes until cooked and flaky. Optionally, add cheese and return for 3 minutes to melt.\", \"Step 1: Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C).\\nStep 2: Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\\nStep 3: Place the seasoned fillets on a baking sheet, optionally sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray.\\nStep 4: Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\\nStep 5: Optionally, after the fish is cooked, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return to the oven for about 3 minutes to allow the cheese to melt.\\n\\nAnswer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350°F (175°C), season the fillets with paprika and black pepper, place them on a baking sheet, and bake for about 15 minutes until the fish is cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, add cheese and return to the oven for 3 minutes to melt.\", \"Step 1: Preheat the oven to the required temperature. Based on passage 2, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C).\\n\\nStep 2: Season the pollock fillets. According to passages 1 and 2, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\\n\\nStep 3: Prepare the baking sheet. As mentioned in passage 1, spray a baking sheet with butter-flavored cooking spray.\\n\\nStep 4: Arrange the fillets. Place the seasoned pollock fillets on the baking sheet. In passage 1, it also suggests layering the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices.\\n\\nStep 5: Bake the fish. According to passages 1, 2, and 3, bake the pollock in the preheated oven until it is cooked and flakes easily, which takes about 15 minutes.\\n\\nStep 6: Optional cheese topping. As described in passage 3, after baking, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return to the oven for about 3 minutes to allow the cheese to melt.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350°F (175°C), season the fillets with paprika and black pepper, optionally place them on a butter-sprayed baking sheet with vegetables, and bake for about 15 minutes until the fish flakes easily. Optionally, add cheese and return to the oven for 3 minutes to melt.\", \"Step 1: Preheat the oven. According to passage 2, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C).\\n\\nStep 2: Season the pollock fillets. As mentioned in both passage 1 and passage 2, season the fillets with paprika and black pepper.\\n\\nStep 3: Prepare the baking sheet. As stated in passage 1, spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray.\\n\\nStep 4: Arrange the fillets and vegetables. Place the seasoned pollock fillets on the baking sheet and layer them with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices (as mentioned in passage 1).\\n\\nStep 5: Bake the fish. According to all three passages, bake in the preheated oven for approximately 15 minutes until the fish is cooked and flakes easily.\\n\\nStep 6: Optional cheese topping. As described in passage 3, after removing the fish from the oven, you can place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return to the oven, and let the cheese melt for about 3 minutes.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To cook pollock, season fillets with paprika and black pepper, place them on a butter-sprayed baking sheet, optionally add vegetables, bake at 350°F (175°C) for about 15 minutes until cooked and flakes easily, and optionally top with cheese to melt for 3 minutes.\"]", "time_sec": 180.737, "generation_time_seconds": 180.7352, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.83, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "39", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, you can run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. Be careful not to damage the dryer's interior. You can then apply 1 to 2 tsp. of nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Afterward, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. You can mix 1 tsp. of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. Some plastic may be extremely difficult to remove, in which case you can try heating the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun and then peel it off. If not all plastic comes off, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer.", "pred": "To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, carefully scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub the area to dissolve any remaining plastic. Wipe the area with a damp cloth, then clean it with a solution of liquid laundry soap and water. Finally, rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth. If the plastic is difficult to remove, use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften it, but if it cannot be fully removed, consider replacing the drum or calling a technician.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the problem — Plastic has melted and stuck to the dryer's interior or drum.\n\nStep 2: Consider the recommended steps from the passage:\n- Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n- Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to gently scrape off the softened plastic, being careful not to damage the dryer’s interior.\n- Apply 1–2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve the plastic.\n- Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover.\n- Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.\n- Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nStep 3: Additional information from the passage:\n- Plastic coating from items like bibs or zip-top bags might melt if high-heat settings are used, but modern dryers have synthetic enamel coatings that resist heat and wear.\n- If the plastic is difficult to remove, heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun may help soften it for peeling.\n- If complete removal is not possible, replacing the drum or entire dryer may be necessary. Professional service is recommended for such repairs.\n\nStep 4: Final answer — Based on the steps provided and the context, the best method to remove melted plastic from a clothes dryer involves softening it with heat, scraping carefully, and using nail polish remover to dissolve the residue, followed by cleaning with soap and water.\n\nAnswer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, carefully scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub the area to dissolve any remaining plastic. Wipe the area with a damp cloth, then clean it with a solution of liquid laundry soap and water. Finally, rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth. If the plastic is difficult to remove, use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften it, but if it cannot be fully removed, consider replacing the drum or calling a technician.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the problem — Plastic has melted and stuck to the dryer's interior or drum.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider the recommended steps from the passage:\\n- Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n- Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to gently scrape off the softened plastic, being careful not to damage the dryer’s interior.\\n- Apply 1–2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve the plastic.\\n- Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover.\\n- Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.\\n- Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\n\\nStep 3: Additional information from the passage:\\n- Plastic coating from items like bibs or zip-top bags might melt if high-heat settings are used, but modern dryers have synthetic enamel coatings that resist heat and wear.\\n- If the plastic is difficult to remove, heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun may help soften it for peeling.\\n- If complete removal is not possible, replacing the drum or entire dryer may be necessary. Professional service is recommended for such repairs.\\n\\nStep 4: Final answer — Based on the steps provided and the context, the best method to remove melted plastic from a clothes dryer involves softening it with heat, scraping carefully, and using nail polish remover to dissolve the residue, followed by cleaning with soap and water.\\n\\nAnswer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, carefully scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub the area to dissolve any remaining plastic. Wipe the area with a damp cloth, then clean it with a solution of liquid laundry soap and water. Finally, rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth. If the plastic is difficult to remove, use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften it, but if it cannot be fully removed, consider replacing the drum or calling a technician.\", \"Step 1: Identify the issue — Plastic has melted inside the dryer, likely from a plastic item like a bib or zip-top bag being accidentally dried.\\n\\nStep 2: Review the options from the passages — Three different sources give methods on how to remove plastic from a dryer.\\n\\nStep 3: Process from passage 1 —\\n- Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n- Use a wooden or plastic scraper to gently remove softened plastic without damaging the dryer.\\n- Apply nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) on a clean cloth and rub the area to dissolve plastic.\\n- Wipe with a damp cloth to rinse the remover.\\n- Clean with a soapy water solution (1 tsp liquid laundry soap + 1 cup water).\\n- Rinse again with a clean, damp cloth.\\n\\nStep 4: Process from passage 2 —\\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic.\\n- Once softened, attempt to peel off the plastic.\\n- If complete removal isn't possible, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer.\\n- Recommend calling a service technician for drum replacement.\\n\\nStep 5: Process from passage 3 —\\n- This passage focuses on removing plastic from dryer coils, not the interior drum or walls. Not directly applicable.\\n\\nStep 6: Combine applicable methods — Based on the most detailed and safe method, passage 1 offers a practical approach without requiring professional tools or disassembly.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run it on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then gently scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the area to dissolve the plastic, followed by wiping with a damp cloth. Clean the area with a solution of liquid laundry soap and water, then rinse thoroughly with clean water.\", \"Step 1: Address the issue of plastic in the dryer.\\nAccording to passage 1, if plastic has melted inside a clothes dryer, one way to remove it is to run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n\\nStep 2: Soften and scrape off the plastic.\\nOnce softened, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer’s interior.\\n\\nStep 3: Use nail polish remover to dissolve remaining residue.\\nApply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve any remaining plastic.\\n\\nStep 4: Rinse with a damp cloth.\\nUse a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\\n\\nStep 5: Clean with a soap and water solution.\\nMix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area. Then rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\n\\nStep 6: Consider alternative solutions.\\nAccording to passage 2, if the plastic is difficult to remove, one can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun and then peeling it off. However, if the plastic cannot be fully removed, replacing the drum or the entire dryer may be necessary.\\n\\nStep 7: Seek professional help if needed.\\nIf the damage is extensive or the plastic cannot be removed entirely, passage 2 recommends calling a service technician to replace the drum or perform repairs.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape it off using a plastic or wooden spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub the area to dissolve any residue, followed by rinsing with a damp cloth. Clean the area with a mixture of liquid laundry soap and water, and rinse again with clean water. If the plastic remains difficult to remove, try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun and peeling it off. If the plastic cannot be fully removed, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer, and contact a professional technician if needed.\", \"Step 1: The passages describe methods for removing plastic that has melted inside a clothes dryer. The first passage gives a detailed step-by-step approach.\\n\\nStep 2: According to passage 1, the first step is to run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n\\nStep 3: After softening, use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to gently scrape off the softened plastic, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior.\\n\\nStep 4: Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it.\\n\\nStep 5: Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\\n\\nStep 6: Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution and rub the area again.\\n\\nStep 7: Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\n\\nStep 8: The second passage suggests an alternative method using a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic so it can be peeled off, but notes that complete removal may not be possible and replacement of the drum may be necessary.\\n\\nStep 9: The third passage refers to removing plastic from dryer coils using a plastic scraper and acetone-based nail polish remover.\\n\\nStep 10: Based on the most complete and relevant instructions, the recommended method includes softening the plastic, scraping, dissolving with nail polish remover, and cleaning with soapy water and rinsing.\\n\\nAnswer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape it off gently using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve the plastic. Wipe with a damp cloth, followed by a cloth dipped in a solution of 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water. Finally, rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\"]", "time_sec": 276.818, "generation_time_seconds": 276.8127, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0002, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 53.49, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "40", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is a brief answer to the question:\n\nTo get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Next, apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse the area with a damp cloth and then mix liquid laundry soap with water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.", "pred": "Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub the area to dissolve the plastic. Rinse with a damp cloth, then clean with a solution of liquid laundry soap and water, followed by a final rinse with clean water. If the plastic is stubborn, heat it with a hair dryer or heat gun and attempt to peel it off. If removal is incomplete, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer and contacting a service technician.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the type of plastic issue. The question asks about removing plastic from a clothes dryer. According to Passage 1, a plastic-coated bib or zip-top bag may melt in the dryer if the high heat setting is used.\n\nStep 2: Follow the instructions from Passage 1. It suggests:\n- Running the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n- Scrape the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper.\n- Apply nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it.\n- Rinse the area with a damp cloth.\n- Use a mixture of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water to rub the area again.\n- Rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth.\n\nStep 3: Consider alternative advice from Passage 2. If the plastic is difficult to remove, heating with a hair dryer or heat gun may soften it enough to peel off. However, if complete removal is not possible, replacing the drum or the entire dryer might be necessary.\n\nStep 4: Combine both sources. The most practical and safe approach is to first try softening the plastic using the dryer on a regular setting (Passage 1), followed by scraping and dissolving with nail polish remover. If that fails, consider heating it with a hair dryer (as mentioned in Passage 2), but if the plastic remains, professional repair or replacement may be required.\n\nFinal Answer: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub the area to dissolve the plastic. Rinse with a damp cloth, then clean with a solution of liquid laundry soap and water, followed by a final rinse with clean water. If the plastic is stubborn, heat it with a hair dryer or heat gun and attempt to peel it off. If removal is incomplete, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer and contacting a service technician.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the type of plastic issue. The question asks about removing plastic from a clothes dryer. According to Passage 1, a plastic-coated bib or zip-top bag may melt in the dryer if the high heat setting is used.\\n\\nStep 2: Follow the instructions from Passage 1. It suggests:\\n- Running the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n- Scrape the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper.\\n- Apply nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it.\\n- Rinse the area with a damp cloth.\\n- Use a mixture of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water to rub the area again.\\n- Rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth.\\n\\nStep 3: Consider alternative advice from Passage 2. If the plastic is difficult to remove, heating with a hair dryer or heat gun may soften it enough to peel off. However, if complete removal is not possible, replacing the drum or the entire dryer might be necessary.\\n\\nStep 4: Combine both sources. The most practical and safe approach is to first try softening the plastic using the dryer on a regular setting (Passage 1), followed by scraping and dissolving with nail polish remover. If that fails, consider heating it with a hair dryer (as mentioned in Passage 2), but if the plastic remains, professional repair or replacement may be required.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub the area to dissolve the plastic. Rinse with a damp cloth, then clean with a solution of liquid laundry soap and water, followed by a final rinse with clean water. If the plastic is stubborn, heat it with a hair dryer or heat gun and attempt to peel it off. If removal is incomplete, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer and contacting a service technician.\", \"Step 1: Identify the relevant information in the passage about removing plastic from a clothes dryer.  \\nFrom passage 1, we know that if plastic is stuck inside the dryer, one can use the following steps:  \\n- Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.  \\n- Scrape the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer’s interior.  \\n- Apply 1–2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub it on the area to dissolve the plastic.  \\n- Rinse the area with a damp cloth to remove the nail polish remover.  \\n- Use a cleaning solution of 1 teaspoon liquid laundry soap mixed with 1 cup water to clean the area again.  \\n- Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.  \\n\\nStep 2: Check if other passages provide additional information.  \\nPassage 2 suggests using a heat gun or hair dryer to soften the plastic, followed by peeling it off. However, it also warns that complete removal may not be possible and that if it fails, replacement of the drum or entire dryer may be necessary.  \\nPassage 3 discusses removing hardened plastic from dryer coils, not the main drum, so it is less relevant.  \\n\\nStep 3: Synthesize the best method based on the most applicable passage.  \\nPassage 1 provides the most direct and actionable method for removing plastic from a dryer.  \\n\\nFinal Answer: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it. Wipe the area with a damp cloth, then clean with a soapy water solution, and finally rinse with clean water.\", \"To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n2. Scrape the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or a paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer’s interior.\\n3. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub it over the plastic to dissolve it.\\n4. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\\n5. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.\\n6. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\n\\nThese steps are effective for removing plastic residue from the dryer drum without causing damage.\", \"Step 1: Identify the problem — Plastic has melted or stuck inside a clothes dryer, likely due to a plastic item (like a bag or bib) being dried.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider the recommended methods from the passage:\\n\\n- Use a regular dryer setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic (from passage 1).\\n- Scrape the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to avoid damaging the dryer interior (passage 1).\\n- Apply nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) to a cloth and rub it onto the plastic to dissolve it (passage 1).\\n- Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\\n- Use a cleaning solution made of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup of water to clean the area again.\\n- Rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth.\\n\\nStep 3: Consider alternative suggestions if the plastic is stubborn:\\n\\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic, making it easier to peel off (passage 2).\\n- If the plastic cannot be removed completely, replacing the drum or the entire dryer might be necessary (passage 2).\\n- For complex repairs like drum replacement, consult a professional technician (passage 2).\\n\\nStep 4: Cross-reference other suggestions:\\n\\n- Passage 3 discusses removing hardened plastic from dryer coils using acetone-based nail polish remover, soaking, scrubbing, and rinsing. This method may be applicable if the plastic is inside the dryer coils.\\n\\nStep 5: Finalize the answer based on the most general and safe approach:\\n\\n- Soften the plastic first (using heat).\\n- Scrape gently with a non-metal tool.\\n- Use nail polish remover to dissolve the plastic.\\n- Clean and rinse thoroughly.\\n- If the plastic is severe or heating doesn’t help, consider professional help or replacement.\\n\\nAnswer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, gently scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. Apply nail polish remover (acetone-based) to a clean cloth and rub it over the residue to dissolve the plastic. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse the remover, then clean with a solution of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water. Rinse again with clean water. If the plastic is stubborn or the damage is significant, use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften it further before peeling\"]", "time_sec": 245.208, "generation_time_seconds": 245.2053, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0004, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.6, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "42", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to remove plastic from a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. (Passage 1)\n2. Heat the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun to make it easier to peel off. Once heated, use a cloth to peel off the plastic. (Passage 2)\n3. Soak a cloth in an acetone-based nail polish remover and use it to scrub the remaining plastic off of the element after leaving it on the element for 5 minutes. Then, wipe the coils with a clean, damp cloth and remove any remaining residue with a cloth dipped in a solution of warm water and dish soap. (Passage 3)\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages. The passages do not provide specific instructions on how to remove plastic from the drum of a clothes dryer.", "pred": "To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve any remaining residue. Clean the area with a damp cloth, followed by a cloth dipped in a solution of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is too stubborn, use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften it and attempt to peel it off. If removal is incomplete, consult a technician to consider replacing the drum.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the problem — Plastic has melted and stuck inside the clothes dryer, likely due to high heat.\n\nStep 2: Consider the instructions from Passage 1 — It suggests running the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scraping it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. Care must be taken not to damage the dryer’s interior.\n\nStep 3: Additional method from Passage 1 — Apply nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) on a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it. Then rinse with a damp cloth, followed by cleaning with a soapy water solution (1 tsp liquid laundry soap in 1 cup water), and rinse again with clean water.\n\nStep 4: Consider alternatives from Passage 2 — If the plastic is stubborn, use a hair dryer or heat gun to warm it up, then try peeling it off. However, complete removal might not be possible, and replacement of the drum or entire dryer might be necessary.\n\nStep 5: Cross-reference Passage 3 — It suggests soaking a cloth in acetone-based nail polish remover and applying it to plastic on dryer coils, scrubbing, and rinsing with soapy water and clean water. This method is more applicable to coils, but it reinforces the effectiveness of nail polish remover for dissolving plastic.\n\nStep 6: Synthesize the best approach — Softening the plastic with heat (either using the dryer or a hair dryer), scraping off the softened plastic with a non-metallic tool, dissolving remaining residue with nail polish remover (acetone-based), and cleaning with soapy water and rinsing with water appears to be the most effective and safe method.\n\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve any remaining residue. Clean the area with a damp cloth, followed by a cloth dipped in a solution of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is too stubborn, use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften it and attempt to peel it off. If removal is incomplete, consult a technician to consider replacing the drum.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the problem — Plastic has melted and stuck inside the clothes dryer, likely due to high heat.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider the instructions from Passage 1 — It suggests running the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scraping it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. Care must be taken not to damage the dryer’s interior.\\n\\nStep 3: Additional method from Passage 1 — Apply nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) on a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it. Then rinse with a damp cloth, followed by cleaning with a soapy water solution (1 tsp liquid laundry soap in 1 cup water), and rinse again with clean water.\\n\\nStep 4: Consider alternatives from Passage 2 — If the plastic is stubborn, use a hair dryer or heat gun to warm it up, then try peeling it off. However, complete removal might not be possible, and replacement of the drum or entire dryer might be necessary.\\n\\nStep 5: Cross-reference Passage 3 — It suggests soaking a cloth in acetone-based nail polish remover and applying it to plastic on dryer coils, scrubbing, and rinsing with soapy water and clean water. This method is more applicable to coils, but it reinforces the effectiveness of nail polish remover for dissolving plastic.\\n\\nStep 6: Synthesize the best approach — Softening the plastic with heat (either using the dryer or a hair dryer), scraping off the softened plastic with a non-metallic tool, dissolving remaining residue with nail polish remover (acetone-based), and cleaning with soapy water and rinsing with water appears to be the most effective and safe method.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve any remaining residue. Clean the area with a damp cloth, followed by a cloth dipped in a solution of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is too stubborn, use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften it and attempt to peel it off. If removal is incomplete, consult a technician to consider replacing the drum.\", \"Step 1: Identify the source of the plastic in the dryer.\\n- The plastic likely comes from items like a plastic-coated bib or zip-top bag that accidentally went into the dryer.\\n\\nStep 2: Understand the type of plastic and how it may have adhered.\\n- If the plastic melted due to high heat, it may have stuck to the dryer's interior.\\n\\nStep 3: Review the recommended cleaning methods.\\n- According to Passage 1, heat the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n- Scrape the softened plastic carefully using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper.\\n- Use a cloth with nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) to dissolve the plastic.\\n- Rinse with a damp cloth to remove the remover.\\n- Clean the area with a solution of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap in 1 cup water.\\n- Rinse again with clean water.\\n\\nStep 4: Consider alternative suggestions.\\n- Passage 2 suggests using a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic so it can be peeled off.\\n- If the plastic does not come off completely, the drum or dryer may need replacement.\\n- It recommends calling a technician for drum replacement.\\n\\nStep 5: Note precautions.\\n- Do not use metal tools to prevent damage to the enamel-coated interior.\\n- Avoid harsh chemicals that may damage the dryer.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Carefully scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the area to dissolve any remaining plastic. Rinse with a damp cloth, then clean with a mixture of liquid laundry soap and water. Rinse again with clean water. If the plastic is stubborn or cannot be fully removed, consider using a heat gun or hair dryer to soften it for peeling. If the plastic persists, the drum or dryer may need to be replaced, and it is recommended to contact a service technician.\", \"Step 1: Identify the issue – Plastic has melted or gotten stuck inside the clothes dryer, likely due to being exposed to high heat.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider the material – The dryer’s interior is coated with synthetic enamel, which is heat-resistant and not easily damaged.\\n\\nStep 3: Evaluate possible solutions – Based on the passage, several methods can be used to remove the plastic.\\n\\nStep 4: Use heat to soften the plastic – According to passage 1, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Alternatively, passage 2 suggests using a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic so it can be peeled off.\\n\\nStep 5: Scrape the softened plastic – Once softened, scrape off the plastic using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper (passage 1). Be careful not to scratch the interior.\\n\\nStep 6: Use chemical removal – Apply nail polish remover (acetone-based) to a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it (passage 1 and 3). Let it sit for a few minutes if needed.\\n\\nStep 7: Clean the area – Wipe the area with a damp cloth to remove residue. Then use a solution of liquid laundry soap and water to clean the surface again (passage 1). For coils, rinse with warm water and dish soap, then rinse thoroughly (passage 3).\\n\\nStep 8: Final rinse – Rinse the area with clean water to remove all residue.\\n\\nStep 9: Consider repair – If the plastic cannot be fully removed or if the drum is damaged, consider replacing the drum or consulting a technician (passage 2).\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular heat setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, gently scrape it off using a plastic or wooden spatula. Apply acetone-based nail polish remover to a cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it. Wipe the area with a damp cloth, then clean again with a soapy water solution. Rinse thoroughly with clean water. If the plastic cannot be fully removed, consult a technician to assess whether the drum needs replacement.\", \"Step 1: Identify the issue — plastic has melted and adhered to the dryer's interior.\\nStep 2: Consider the method from Passage 1: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\nStep 3: Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to gently scrape the softened plastic off, avoiding damage to the dryer.\\nStep 4: Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth (1–2 tsp), and rub the area to dissolve the plastic.\\nStep 5: Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\\nStep 6: Mix 1 tsp liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water and use a clean cloth dipped in this solution to rub the area again.\\nStep 7: Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\nStep 8: Note from Passage 1 that modern dryers have heat-resistant synthetic enamel coatings, so minor plastic damage is not typically serious.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Scrape it off gently using a wooden or plastic tool. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth to dissolve the remaining plastic, then wipe the area with a damp cloth. Clean the area again with a solution of liquid laundry soap and water, and rinse thoroughly with clean water.\"]", "time_sec": 272.647, "generation_time_seconds": 272.6439, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0002, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.57, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "43", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to get plastic out of a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\n4. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nIf the plastic is still difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun before attempting to scrape it off. However, if the plastic cannot be removed completely, it may be necessary to replace the drum or the entire dryer.", "pred": "Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, gently scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to remove the remover, then clean with a solution of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water. Rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the issue — Plastic has melted inside the dryer and needs to be removed.\n\nStep 2: Refer to passage 1 for instructions on how to remove plastic from a dryer. It suggests:\n- Running the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n- Scraping the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper.\n- Being cautious not to damage the dryer’s interior.\n- Applying nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) to a clean cloth to dissolve the plastic.\n- Wiping the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover.\n- Using a mixture of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water to clean the area again.\n- Rinsing thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nStep 3: Passage 2 mentions an alternative method involving heating with a hair dryer or heat gun to soften the plastic, followed by peeling. However, it also notes that complete removal may not be possible and suggests replacing the drum or the entire dryer if the plastic cannot be fully removed.\n\nStep 4: Passage 3 is specific to removing plastic from dryer coils (not the drum), so it is not directly relevant for general dryer interior cleaning.\n\nStep 5: Since the question specifically asks how to remove plastic from a clothes dryer, and passage 1 provides a detailed, step-by-step guide to clean the dryer’s interior, this is the most appropriate method.\n\nFinal Answer: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, gently scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to remove the remover, then clean with a solution of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water. Rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the issue — Plastic has melted inside the dryer and needs to be removed.\\n\\nStep 2: Refer to passage 1 for instructions on how to remove plastic from a dryer. It suggests:\\n- Running the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n- Scraping the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper.\\n- Being cautious not to damage the dryer’s interior.\\n- Applying nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) to a clean cloth to dissolve the plastic.\\n- Wiping the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover.\\n- Using a mixture of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water to clean the area again.\\n- Rinsing thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\n\\nStep 3: Passage 2 mentions an alternative method involving heating with a hair dryer or heat gun to soften the plastic, followed by peeling. However, it also notes that complete removal may not be possible and suggests replacing the drum or the entire dryer if the plastic cannot be fully removed.\\n\\nStep 4: Passage 3 is specific to removing plastic from dryer coils (not the drum), so it is not directly relevant for general dryer interior cleaning.\\n\\nStep 5: Since the question specifically asks how to remove plastic from a clothes dryer, and passage 1 provides a detailed, step-by-step guide to clean the dryer’s interior, this is the most appropriate method.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, gently scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to remove the remover, then clean with a solution of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water. Rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth.\", \"Step 1: Identify the issue – Plastic has melted or adhered inside the dryer, likely due to a plastic item like a bag or bib being exposed to high heat.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider the solution – The passages suggest two methods: one for surface-level plastic removal and another for more stubborn or embedded plastic.\\n\\nStep 3: Follow the instructions from Passage 1 – This method is suitable for normal dryer interiors:\\n- Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n- Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to carefully remove softened plastic.\\n- Apply nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) to a clean cloth and rub it onto the remaining plastic to dissolve it.\\n- Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover.\\n- Use a solution of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap in 1 cup water to clean the area again.\\n- Rinse thoroughly with a clean, damp cloth.\\n\\nStep 4: Consider Passage 2 – If the plastic is harder to remove or has fused with the dryer drum:\\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic, making it easier to peel off.\\n- If peeling doesn’t remove all the plastic, the drum may need to be replaced.\\n- Recommended: Hire a service technician for drum replacement.\\n\\nStep 5: Combine the methods – Begin with the gentle method from Passage 1. If unsuccessful, try heating and peeling with a hair dryer or heat gun as in Passage 2. If the plastic remains or the drum is damaged, replacement may be necessary.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off using a plastic or wooden spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth to dissolve any residue, wipe with a damp cloth, and clean with a soapy water solution before rinsing. For tougher plastic, use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften it before peeling. If the plastic cannot be fully removed or the drum is damaged, consider replacing the drum by contacting a service technician.\", \"Step 1: Identify the issue – Plastic residue from a plastic-coated item (like a bib or zip-top bag) that has melted inside the dryer.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider the first method (from Passage 1):\\n- Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n- Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to gently scrape off the softened plastic, avoiding damage to the dryer’s interior.\\n- Apply 1–2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the area to dissolve any remaining plastic.\\n- Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\\n- Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water and use this solution on a cloth to clean the area again.\\n- Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\n\\nStep 3: Consider a second method (from Passage 2):\\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic coating inside the dryer.\\n- Once softened, try peeling the plastic off manually.\\n- If not all plastic can be removed, the drum may need to be replaced, and a service technician is recommended.\\n\\nStep 4: Compare methods – Passage 1 provides a step-by-step cleaning method using heat, scraping, and chemical removal with nail polish remover. Passage 2 suggests heating and peeling, but warns that full removal might not be possible and replacement could be necessary.\\n\\nStep 5: Combine the best approaches – For most cases, starting with gentle heating to soften the plastic, followed by careful scraping and cleaning with nail polish remover and soapy water (as in Passage 1) is effective. If the plastic is hard to remove or the damage is extensive, professional help or replacement may be required.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, gently scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub the area to dissolve any remaining residue, followed by wiping with a damp cloth. Clean the area again with a soapy water solution (1 tsp laundry soap in 1 cup water), and rinse thoroughly with clean water. If the plastic is stubborn or the drum is damaged, consider contacting a service technician to assess whether the drum needs replacement.\", \"Step 1: Identify the problem – Plastic has melted and adhered to the dryer interior (possibly drum or coils), and we need to remove it.\\n\\nStep 2: Refer to Passage 1 – It describes steps to remove softened plastic from a dryer:\\n- Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n- Scrape off the softened plastic using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, avoiding damage to the dryer’s interior.\\n- Apply nail polish remover (1–2 tsp) to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it.\\n- Clean the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover.\\n- Use a mixture of 1 tsp liquid laundry soap and 1 cup water to wash the area.\\n- Rinse thoroughly with a clean cloth dipped in clean water.\\n\\nStep 3: Refer to Passage 2 – This passage suggests an alternative:\\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften the plastic.\\n- Attempt to peel off the softened plastic.\\n- If not fully removable, the drum may need replacement, and a service technician should be contacted.\\n\\nStep 4: Refer to Passage 3 – This passage describes removing plastic from dryer coils:\\n- Scrape hardened plastic with a plastic scraper.\\n- Soak a cloth in acetone-based nail polish remover and let it sit on the coil for 5 minutes.\\n- Scrub remaining plastic with the cloth.\\n- Wipe with a damp cloth.\\n- Clean residue with a cloth dipped in warm water and dish soap.\\n- Rinse with water.\\n\\nStep 5: Synthesize – Based on the instructions, the best way to remove plastic from a dryer involves:\\n- Softening the plastic using heat (dryer, hair dryer, or heat gun).\\n- Scraping or peeling off the softened plastic.\\n- Dissolving remaining residue with nail polish remover (preferably acetone-based).\\n- Cleaning with soapy water and rinsing.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first soften the plastic by running the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes or using a hair dryer. Then, gently scrape it off with a plastic or wooden spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth to dissolve any remaining residue, followed by cleaning with a cloth dampened with a mixture of dish soap and warm water, and finally rinse with clean water.\"]", "time_sec": 296.631, "generation_time_seconds": 296.6283, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 53.59, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "44", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to remove plastic from a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it.\n4. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\n5. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again.\n6. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nIf the plastic does not come off with this method, another option is to use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic until it becomes soft and pliable. Then, attempt to peel it off carefully. However, be aware that this method may not work, and in that case, replacing the drum or the entire dryer may be necessary. It is recommended to call a service technician for this type of repair.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question of how to get plastic out of clothes dryer is:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper.\n3. Apply nail polish remover to dissolve the plastic.\n4. Rinse with a damp cloth and soap solution.\n5. Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic if necessary.\n\nIf the plastic still does not come off, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer.", "pred": "To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then gently scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover on a cloth to dissolve any remaining residue, followed by wiping the area with a damp cloth. Clean the area again with a mixture of liquid laundry soap and water, and rinse with clean water. Alternatively, use a heat gun or hair dryer to heat the plastic and peel it off. If the plastic cannot be fully removed or the drum is damaged, consider replacing the drum by contacting a service technician.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the issue — Plastic has melted and stuck inside the dryer, possibly on the interior walls or drum.\n\nStep 2: Consider the instructions for removing soft plastic from dryer interior:\n- Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n- Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to gently scrape off softened plastic.\n- Be cautious not to damage the dryer’s interior.\n\nStep 3: If plastic is still present, apply nail polish remover:\n- Use 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover on a clean cloth.\n- Rub the area to dissolve any remaining plastic residue.\n- Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover.\n\nStep 4: Clean with a soap solution:\n- Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water.\n- Dip a clean cloth into the solution and rub the area.\n- Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nStep 5: Consider alternative method — heating:\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic to make it easier to peel off.\n- The plastic may not come off completely.\n- If it cannot be fully removed, the drum may need to be replaced.\n\nStep 6: Final advice:\n- If plastic cannot be fully removed or if the drum is damaged, consult a professional technician for possible drum replacement.\n\nAnswer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then gently scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover on a cloth to dissolve any remaining residue, followed by wiping the area with a damp cloth. Clean the area again with a mixture of liquid laundry soap and water, and rinse with clean water. Alternatively, use a heat gun or hair dryer to heat the plastic and peel it off. If the plastic cannot be fully removed or the drum is damaged, consider replacing the drum by contacting a service technician.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the issue — Plastic has melted and stuck inside the dryer, possibly on the interior walls or drum.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider the instructions for removing soft plastic from dryer interior:\\n- Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\n- Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to gently scrape off softened plastic.\\n- Be cautious not to damage the dryer’s interior.\\n\\nStep 3: If plastic is still present, apply nail polish remover:\\n- Use 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover on a clean cloth.\\n- Rub the area to dissolve any remaining plastic residue.\\n- Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover.\\n\\nStep 4: Clean with a soap solution:\\n- Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water.\\n- Dip a clean cloth into the solution and rub the area.\\n- Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\n\\nStep 5: Consider alternative method — heating:\\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic to make it easier to peel off.\\n- The plastic may not come off completely.\\n- If it cannot be fully removed, the drum may need to be replaced.\\n\\nStep 6: Final advice:\\n- If plastic cannot be fully removed or if the drum is damaged, consult a professional technician for possible drum replacement.\\n\\nAnswer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then gently scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover on a cloth to dissolve any remaining residue, followed by wiping the area with a damp cloth. Clean the area again with a mixture of liquid laundry soap and water, and rinse with clean water. Alternatively, use a heat gun or hair dryer to heat the plastic and peel it off. If the plastic cannot be fully removed or the drum is damaged, consider replacing the drum by contacting a service technician.\", \"Step 1: Identify the source of the plastic in the dryer.  \\n- The passage suggests that a plastic item, such as a bib or zip-top bag, may have melted inside the dryer.\\n\\nStep 2: Assess the method for removing the plastic.  \\n- One method involves running the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.  \\n- Then, use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to gently scrape the softened plastic off, avoiding damage to the dryer’s interior.  \\n\\nStep 3: Consider chemical removal methods.  \\n- Apply 1–2 teaspoons of nail polish remover (acetone-based) to a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it.  \\n- Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the remover.  \\n- Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.  \\n- Rinse thoroughly with a clean cloth dipped in clean water.\\n\\nStep 4: Evaluate alternative approaches.  \\n- If the plastic is very stubborn, use a hair dryer or heat gun to soften it further, then try peeling it off.  \\n- If the plastic cannot be fully removed, the dryer drum may need to be replaced, which should be done by a professional technician.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, run the dryer for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then gently scrape it off using a wooden or plastic tool. Alternatively, apply acetone-based nail polish remover with a cloth to dissolve the plastic, followed by cleaning with a soapy water solution and rinsing thoroughly. If the plastic is difficult to remove, use heat from a hair dryer to soften it before peeling; if unsuccessful, consider replacing the drum and consult a service technician.\", \"Step 1: According to Passage 1, the first step is to run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\nStep 2: After softening, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to avoid damaging the dryer’s interior.\\nStep 3: Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve the plastic.\\nStep 4: Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\\nStep 5: Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth into the solution, and rub the area again.\\nStep 6: Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\nStep 7: According to Passage 2, an alternative approach is to use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic, which may allow it to be peeled off.\\nStep 8: If the plastic cannot be removed completely, the drum or entire dryer might need to be replaced, and a service technician should be contacted.\\nStep 9: Passage 3 provides further guidance for removing plastic from dryer coils using a plastic scraper and acetone-based nail polish remover, followed by cleaning with warm water and dish soap.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To remove plastic from a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, carefully scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula. Apply nail polish remover to a cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it, followed by wiping with a damp cloth. Clean the area with a mixture of liquid laundry soap and water, and rinse thoroughly with clean water. Alternatively, heat the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun and attempt to peel it off. If not fully removable, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer and consult a professional technician.\", \"Step 1: Identify the relevant information from the passage regarding plastic in a clothes dryer.\\n\\nFrom Passage 1:\\n- \\\"Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\\\"\\n- \\\"Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\\\"\\n- \\\"Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it.\\\"\\n- \\\"Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\\\"\\n- \\\"Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again.\\\"\\n- \\\"Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\\\"\\n\\nThese steps outline a method to remove softened plastic from a dryer’s interior.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider if there are any other sources of information.\\n\\nPassage 2 discusses removing plastic from a dryer drum, suggesting heating with a hair dryer or heat gun and then peeling off the plastic. It notes that complete removal may not be possible, and if not, drum replacement may be necessary.\\n\\nPassage 3 discusses removing hardened plastic from dryer coils, not the main drum or interior.\\n\\nStep 3: Determine the most appropriate method based on the question.\\n\\nSince the question is about removing plastic from a clothes dryer (general), and Passage 1 provides detailed steps for the interior of a dryer, this is the most directly applicable information.\\n\\nFinal Answer:\\nAnswer: Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, carefully scrape it off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. Next, apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub it on the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse with a damp cloth, then clean the area with a mixture of liquid laundry soap and water. Finally, rinse thoroughly with a clean, water-dampened cloth.\"]", "time_sec": 262.097, "generation_time_seconds": 262.0925, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 42.35, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "46", "question": "how to fold a quilt", "gt": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "This method helps ensure the quilt is folded neatly and precisely, preserving the alignment of the fabric layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To fold a quilt, follow these steps:\n\n1. Lay the quilt flat on a bed, table, or another flat surface.\n2. Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is made on the bias.\n3. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, again making sure the fold is on the bias.\n4. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, maintaining the bias fold.\n5. Finally, fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring this fold is also on the bias.\n\nThis method helps ensure the quilt is folded neatly and precisely, preserving the alignment of the fabric layers.", "cot": "[\"To fold a quilt, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Lay the quilt flat on a bed, table, or another flat surface.\\n2. Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is made on the bias.\\n3. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, again making sure the fold is on the bias.\\n4. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, maintaining the bias fold.\\n5. Finally, fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring this fold is also on the bias.\\n\\nThis method helps ensure the quilt is folded neatly and precisely, preserving the alignment of the fabric layers.\", \"Step 1: Lay the quilt on a flat surface such as a bed or table.  \\nStep 2: Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\nStep 3: Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, also ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\nStep 4: Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, maintaining the bias fold.  \\nStep 5: Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, again making sure the fold is on the bias.\\n\\nThese steps are part of preparing the quilt for mitered binding as described in the passage.\", \"Step 1: Lay the quilt on a flat surface such as a bed or table.\\nStep 2: Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\\nStep 3: Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, again making sure the fold is on the bias.\\nStep 4: Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, maintaining a bias fold.\\nStep 5: Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\\n\\nThese steps describe how to fold the corners of the quilt toward the center in a sequence that helps square up the quilt and prepare it for binding.\\n\\nAnswer: Fold the lower right corner up toward the center, then fold the lower left corner up, followed by folding the top left corner down, and finally folding the top right corner down, ensuring each fold is made on the bias.\", \"Step 1: Lay your quilt on a flat surface such as a bed or table.  \\nStep 2: Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\nStep 3: Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, making sure the fold is on the bias.  \\nStep 4: Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, again ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \\nStep 5: Finally, fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, maintaining a bias fold.\\n\\nAnswer: To fold a quilt, fold each of its four corners toward the center—lower right, lower left, top left, and top right—ensuring each fold is made on the bias.\"]", "time_sec": 114.0, "generation_time_seconds": 113.9956, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0004, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
