{"id": "119", "question": "teresa name meaning", "gt": "The name Teresa is of Greek, American, Italian, and Spanish origin. In Greek and American, it means 'Reap; from Therasia.' In Italian, it is interpreted as 'Harvester.' In Spanish, the name is associated with two popular saints, 'Teresa of Avila' and 'Therese of Lisieux.' The name is also thought to originate from the Greek “therizein” meaning “to harvest, harvester” or from the Greek “theros” meaning “summer”.", "pred": "The name Teresa is derived from the Greek word \"therizein\", meaning \"to harvest\", so its primary meaning is \"harvester\" or \"reaper\". It may also be linked to \"theros\", meaning \"summer\", or from the Greek island Therasia.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Let's analyze the information provided in the passages:\n\n- Passage 1 states that in Greek, the meaning of \"Teresa\" is \"Reap; from Therasia\".\n- It also mentions that in Italian, the meaning is \"Harvester\".\n- In Spanish, the meaning is given as \"Name of two popular saints 'Teresa of Avila' and 'Therese of Lisieux'\".\n- Passage 2 provides the meaning of \"Theresa\" (a variant spelling) as \"Reaper; from Therasia\" in Greek and American contexts.\n- Passage 3 discusses the etymology and suggests that \"Teresa\" comes from the Greek \"therizein\", meaning \"to harvest, harvester\", or from the Greek \"theros\", meaning \"summer\". It also notes that Therasia is a Greek island.\n\nFrom these sources:\n- Multiple sources confirm that the name \"Teresa\" has Greek roots and is associated with the word \"harvest\" or \"harvester\".\n- The meaning appears to be linked to the Greek verb \"therizein\", which means \"to harvest\".\n- The name may also derive from \"theros\", meaning \"summer\", or from the island Therasia.\n\nTherefore, the meaning of the name Teresa is primarily \"harvester\" or \"reaper\", with roots in Greek etymology.\n\nAnswer: The 