vester,\\\" and in American contexts, it is interpreted as \\\"Reap; from Therasia.\\\" In Spanish, it is the name of two popular saints, Teresa of Ávila and Thérèse of Lisieux. The name's origin is thought to come from the Greek island of Therasia or from the Greek word \\\"theros,\\\" meaning \\\"summer.\\\"\", \"Step 1: Analyze the Greek meaning of the name Teresa.\\n- According to passage 1, in Greek, the meaning of Teresa is \\\"Reap; from Therasia.\\\"\\n- According to passage 2, in Greek, the meaning of Theresa is \\\"Reaper; from Therasia.\\\"\\n- This suggests that the name Teresa (or Theresa) is derived from \\\"Therasia\\\" and has a meaning related to reaping or harvesting.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider the Italian and Spanish meanings.\\n- Passage 1 states that in Italian, the meaning of Teresa is \\\"Harvester.\\\"\\n- In Spanish, the meaning is linked to two saints: Teresa of Avila and Therese of Lisieux.\\n\\nStep 3: Consider the etymology and historical origin.\\n- Passage 3 explains that Teresa is the Spanish, Portuguese, and Italian form of Theresa.\\n- It suggests that the name comes from the Greek word \\\"therizein,\\\" meaning \\\"to harvest, harvester.\\\"\\n- It also mentions that \\\"theros\\\" means \\\"summer,\\\" indicating another possible origin.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe meaning of the name Teresa is generally associated with harvesting or reaping, derived from Greek roots. It is also linked to the name of a Greek island, Therasia.\\n\\nFinal Answer: The name Teresa has multiple meanings across different cultures. In Greek, it means \\\"reap\\\" or \\\"reaper,\\\" derived from Therasia. In Italian, it means \\\"harvester.\\\" In Spanish, it is associated with two saints. Etymologically, it may come from the Greek word \\\"therizein,\\\" meaning \\\"to harvest.\\\"\", \"Let's analyze the meaning of the name \\\"Teresa\\\" step by step based on the provided passages:\\n\\n1. Greek Meaning:\\n   - According to Passage 1, in Greek, the meaning of the name Teresa is \\\"Reap; from Therasia.\\\"\\n   - According to Passage 2, in Greek, the name Theresa is a variant, and its meaning is \\\"Reaper; from Therasia.\\\"\\n   - Passage 3 suggests that Teresa may come from the Greek \\\"therizein,\\\" meaning \\\"to harvest, harvester.\\\"\\n\\n2. Italian Meaning:\\n   - Passage 1 states that in Italian, Teresa means \\\"Harvester.\\\"\\n\\n3. Spanish Meaning:\\n   - Passage 1 explains that in Spanish, Teresa is the name of two popular saints: \\\"Teresa of Avila\\\" and \\\"Therese of Lisieux.\\\"\\n\\n4. American Meaning:\\n   - Passage 1 states that in American usage, Teresa means \\\"Reap; from Therasia.\\\"\\n\\n5. Etymology:\\n   - Passage 3 explains that Teresa is the Spanish, Portuguese, and Italian form of Theresa, with uncertain etymology.\\n   - It is thought to derive from the Greek \\\"therizein\\\" (meaning \\\"to harvest, harvester\\\") or from \\\"theros\\\" (meaning \\\"summer\\\").\\n   - \\\"Therasia\\\" is also mentioned as a Greek island, possibly linked to the name.\\n\\nIn summary, the name Teresa carries meanings related to harvesting or reaping in multiple cultures, particularly in Greek (\\\"to harvest, harvester\\\") and Italian (\\\"Harvester\\\"). It is also associated with saints in Spanish culture and has roots in pre-Middle Ages Spain and Portugal.\\n\\nFinal Answer: The name Teresa is derived from Greek roots meaning \\\"to harvest\\\" or \\\"harvester,\\\" and in Italian, it means \\\"Harvester.\\\" In Spanish, it is associated with saints like Teresa of Avila and Thérèse of Lisieux, while in American and Greek contexts, it is linked to the idea of \\\"reaping.\\\"\", \"Step 1: Analyze the passages for consistent information about the meaning of the name Teresa.\\n\\nPassage 1 indicates the following meanings:\\n- Greek: \\\"Reap; from Therasia\\\"\\n- American: \\\"Reap; from Therasia\\\"\\n- Italian: \\\"Harvester\\\"\\n- Spanish: \\\"Name of two popular saints 'Teresa of Avila' and 'Therese of Lisieux'\\\"\\n\\nPassage 2 provides meanings for the variant \\\"Theresa\\\":\\n- Greek: \\\"Reaper; from Therasia\\\"\\n- American: \\\"Reaper; from Therasia\\\"\\n\\nPassage 3 discusses the etymology:\\n- From Greek \\\"therizein\\\" meaning \\\"to harvest, harvester\\\"\\n- From Greek \\\"theros\\\" meaning \\\"summer\\\"\\n- Also linked to Therasia, a Greek island\\n\\nStep 2: Combine and reconcile the meanings across all sources.\\n\\n- The Greek root \\\"therizein\\\" consistently supports meanings like \\\"to harvest\\\" or \\\"harvester.\\\"\\n- \\\"Reap\\\" and \\\"Reaper\\\" are synonyms for \\\"harvester,\\\" aligning with the Greek etymology.\\n- The Spanish meaning references saints, but this is more about cultural usage than the literal meaning.\\n\\nStep 3: Determine the core meaning of Teresa.\\n\\nBased on the Greek etymology and multiple language references, the primary meaning of Teresa is connected to \\\"harvesting.\\\" This aligns with the Greek terms \\\"therizein\\\" (to harvest) and \\\"theros\\\" (summer, associated with harvest time).\\n\\nFinal Answer: The name Teresa primarily means \\\"harvester\\\" or \\\"reaper,\\\" derived from the Greek word \\\"therizein,\\\" meaning \\\"to harvest.\\\"\"]", "time_sec": 235.469, "generation_time_seconds": 235.4659, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0002, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 44.04, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "124", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4 x 4 skids to a length of 70 2/4\" and set and level them according to the shed building plans. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4\" and six joists at 44 3/4\". Assemble the floor and place it on the skids as indicated in the floor framing section of the plans. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles. Cut two more 4 x 4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids and, when the frame is square, toenail the floor joists to the skids. Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. The walls for the shed are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. The size of the shed can be adjusted as needed.", "pred": "Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel, cutting and positioning 4x4 skids (either to 6 feet or 70 2/4 inches), constructing the floor frame with rim joists and joists, anchoring the frame to the skids using joist clip angles or toenailing, attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, and finally raising pre-built walls onto the frame. The shed", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the key steps involved in building a lean-to shed based on the provided passages.\n\n- Passage 1 mentions preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\n- Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set and level them.\n- Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\n- Assemble the floor frame and set it on the skids.\n- Check for squareness and anchor the frame using joist clip angles.\n\n- Passage 2 states:\n- Cut two pressure-treated 4x4s to 6 feet for the skids.\n- Position the frame on the skids and toenail the floor joists to the skids.\n- Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring.\n- Walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame.\n\n- Passage 3 notes:\n- The shed dimensions are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\n- You can expand dimensions in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\n- Cut two pressure-treated 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\n\nStep 2: Synthesize the information across all three passages.\n\n- Skids: Made from 4x4 pressure-treated wood, cut to a length of 6 feet or 70 2/4 inches depending on the plan.\n- Floor: Constructed from rim joists and joists, assembled and set on skids.\n- Anchoring: Use joist clip angles or toenailing to secure the frame.\n- Flooring: Add 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood.\n- Walls: Built separately and then raised onto the floor frame.\n- Site preparation: Use 4 inches of compacted gravel.\n- Dimensions: Base size is 6 feet by 4 feet, expandable.\n\nStep 3: Finalize the answer based on the synthesis.\n\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel, cutting and positioning 4x4 skids (either to 6 feet or 70 2/4 inches), constructing the floor frame with rim joists and joists, anchoring the frame to the skids using joist clip angles or toenailing, attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, and finally raising pre-built walls onto the frame. The shed", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the key steps involved in building a lean-to shed based on the provided passages.\\n\\n- Passage 1 mentions preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n- Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set and level them.\\n- Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\\n- Assemble the floor frame and set it on the skids.\\n- Check for squareness and anchor the frame using joist clip angles.\\n\\n- Passage 2 states:\\n- Cut two pressure-treated 4x4s to 6 feet for the skids.\\n- Position the frame on the skids and toenail the floor joists to the skids.\\n- Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring.\\n- Walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame.\\n\\n- Passage 3 notes:\\n- The shed dimensions are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\n- You can expand dimensions in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n- Cut two pressure-treated 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\\n\\nStep 2: Synthesize the information across all three passages.\\n\\n- Skids: Made from 4x4 pressure-treated wood, cut to a length of 6 feet or 70 2/4 inches depending on the plan.\\n- Floor: Constructed from rim joists and joists, assembled and set on skids.\\n- Anchoring: Use joist clip angles or toenailing to secure the frame.\\n- Flooring: Add 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood.\\n- Walls: Built separately and then raised onto the floor frame.\\n- Site preparation: Use 4 inches of compacted gravel.\\n- Dimensions: Base size is 6 feet by 4 feet, expandable.\\n\\nStep 3: Finalize the answer based on the synthesis.\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel, cutting and positioning 4x4 skids (either to 6 feet or 70 2/4 inches), constructing the floor frame with rim joists and joists, anchoring the frame to the skids using joist clip angles or toenailing, attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, and finally raising pre-built walls onto the frame. The shed\", \"Step 1: Site preparation – The first step in building a lean-to shed is preparing the site. This involves laying a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel to ensure a stable and level foundation.\\n\\nStep 2: Skid installation – Cut two 4x4 skids to the required length. In some plans, these are cut to 70 2/4 inches, while others use 6-foot-long skids. These skids serve as the base supports for the shed frame.\\n\\nStep 3: Floor framing – Cut two rim joists and several floor joists to the specified lengths (for example, 7 3/4 inches for rim joists and 44 3/4 inches for joists in one plan, or 6 feet for rim joists in another). Assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids, ensuring it is level and square.\\n\\nStep 4: Anchoring the frame – Once the floor frame is in place and squared, anchor it to the skids using joist clip angles or toenail the joists to the skids as per the plan.\\n\\nStep 5: Floor decking – Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring over the frame to complete the floor.\\n\\nStep 6: Wall construction – The walls are typically built separately on the ground before being raised and attached to the floor frame.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To build a lean-to shed, begin by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel, then install skids cut to size. Assemble the floor frame using rim joists and joists, place it on the skids, level and square it, and anchor it securely. Attach plywood flooring, and then construct and raise the walls onto the frame.\", \"Step 1: Site Preparation\\n- Prepare the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel.\\n\\nStep 2: Skids\\n- Cut two 4 x 4 skids to a length of 70 2/4″ (as per passage 1), or alternatively, cut two pressure-treated 4 by 4s to a length of 6 feet (as per passage 2).\\n- Set and level the skids following the shed building plans.\\n\\nStep 3: Floor Framing\\n- Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists to 7 3/4″ and six joists to 44 3/4″ (passage 1).\\n- Alternatively, cut two pressure-treated 2 by 4s to 6 feet for the rim joists (passage 3).\\n- Assemble the floor frame and set it on the skids, checking for squareness.\\n\\nStep 4: Anchoring and Flooring\\n- Anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles (passage 1).\\n- Alternatively, toenail the floor joists to the skids when square (passage 2).\\n- Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame (passage 2).\\n\\nStep 5: Wall Construction\\n- Construct the walls separately and then raise them onto the frame (passage 2).\\n\\nStep 6: Dimensions\\n- The shed shown is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet (passage 3).\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with compacted gravel, installing skids, assembling and anchoring the floor frame, attaching plywood flooring, and raising pre-constructed walls; dimensions can be adjusted in 2 or 4-foot increments.\", \"Step 1: Site Preparation\\n- Prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n\\nStep 2: Skid Installation\\n- Cut two 4x4 skids to the required length (either 70 2/4 inches as per passage 1 or 6 feet as per passage 2).\\n- Set and level the skids according to the shed building plans.\\n\\nStep 3: Floor Framing\\n- Cut two rim joists (either 2x6 or 2x4 depending on the passage) to the appropriate length (7 3/4 inches or 6 feet respectively).\\n- Cut floor joists to the required length (44 3/4 inches as per passage 1).\\n- Assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids.\\n- Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using joist clip angles or toenailing as described.\\n\\nStep 4: Floor Decking\\n- Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the floor frame.\\n\\nStep 5: Wall Construction\\n- Construct the walls separately.\\n- Raise the walls onto the floor frame.\\n\\nStep 6: Shed Dimensions\\n- The shed can be built to standard dimensions such as 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, or expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with compacted gravel, installing skids, assembling and anchoring the floor frame, adding plywood decking, and raising pre-constructed walls. The shed dimensions can be customized, typically starting at 6 feet by 4 feet.\"]", "time_sec": 262.415, "generation_time_seconds": 262.412, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 44.29, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "125", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the process of building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with compacted gravel, cutting and setting the 4 x 4 skids, cutting and assembling the floor with 2 x 6 rim joists and joists, anchoring the frame to the skids, attaching tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, constructing the walls separately, and raising them onto the frame.", "pred": "To build a lean-to shed, prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut skids (either 4x4s to 70 2/4 inches or 6 feet) and set them level. Assemble the floor frame using rim joists and joists, anchor it to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. Finally, construct walls separately and raise them onto the frame. The shed dimensions can be customized, commonly starting at 6 feet by 4 feet with possible expansions in 2 or 4-foot increments.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Analyze the provided passages for information about building a lean-to shed.\n- Passage 1 mentions preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\n- It instructs cutting two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and setting them level.\n- It also discusses cutting rim joists and joists for the floor, assembling the floor, and anchoring it with joist clip angles.\n\n- Passage 2 mentions cutting pressure-treated 4x4s to 6 feet for skids.\n- It instructs positioning the frame on the skids, toenailing floor joists, and attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring.\n- It also notes that walls are built separately and then raised.\n\n- Passage 3 specifies dimensions of a shed (6 feet long by 4 feet wide) and suggests expanding dimensions in 2 or 4-foot increments.\n- It mentions cutting two pressure-treated 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\n\nStep 2: Combine relevant information to summarize the steps for building a lean-to shed.\n- Prepare the site with gravel (4-inch layer).\n- Cut skids (either 4x4s to 70 2/4 inches or 6 feet depending on the plan).\n- Set and level skids.\n- Assemble floor frame using rim joists and joists.\n- Anchor floor frame to skids.\n- Attach plywood flooring.\n- Build walls separately and raise them onto the floor frame.\n\nStep 3: Provide a concise and accurate answer based on the synthesis of all relevant information.\nAnswer: To build a lean-to shed, prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut skids (either 4x4s to 70 2/4 inches or 6 feet) and set them level. Assemble the floor frame using rim joists and joists, anchor it to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. Finally, construct walls separately and raise them onto the frame. The shed dimensions can be customized, commonly starting at 6 feet by 4 feet with possible expansions in 2 or 4-foot increments.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Analyze the provided passages for information about building a lean-to shed.\\n- Passage 1 mentions preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n- It instructs cutting two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and setting them level.\\n- It also discusses cutting rim joists and joists for the floor, assembling the floor, and anchoring it with joist clip angles.\\n\\n- Passage 2 mentions cutting pressure-treated 4x4s to 6 feet for skids.\\n- It instructs positioning the frame on the skids, toenailing floor joists, and attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring.\\n- It also notes that walls are built separately and then raised.\\n\\n- Passage 3 specifies dimensions of a shed (6 feet long by 4 feet wide) and suggests expanding dimensions in 2 or 4-foot increments.\\n- It mentions cutting two pressure-treated 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\\n\\nStep 2: Combine relevant information to summarize the steps for building a lean-to shed.\\n- Prepare the site with gravel (4-inch layer).\\n- Cut skids (either 4x4s to 70 2/4 inches or 6 feet depending on the plan).\\n- Set and level skids.\\n- Assemble floor frame using rim joists and joists.\\n- Anchor floor frame to skids.\\n- Attach plywood flooring.\\n- Build walls separately and raise them onto the floor frame.\\n\\nStep 3: Provide a concise and accurate answer based on the synthesis of all relevant information.\\nAnswer: To build a lean-to shed, prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut skids (either 4x4s to 70 2/4 inches or 6 feet) and set them level. Assemble the floor frame using rim joists and joists, anchor it to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. Finally, construct walls separately and raise them onto the frame. The shed dimensions can be customized, commonly starting at 6 feet by 4 feet with possible expansions in 2 or 4-foot increments.\", \"Let's analyze the provided passages step by step to answer the question about building a lean-to shed:\\n\\n1. Passage 1 provides detailed instructions for building a lean-to shed:\\n   - Site preparation: A 4″ layer of compacted gravel is required.\\n   - Skids: Two 4 x 4 skids are cut to 70 2/4″ and must be set and leveled.\\n   - Floor frame: Two 2 x 6 rim joists are cut at 7 3/4″, and six joists at 44 3/4″.\\n   - Assemble the floor frame and set it on the skids.\\n   - Check for squareness and anchor the frame using four joist clip angles.\\n\\n2. Passage 2 adds:\\n   - Skids: Cut two pressure-treated 4 x 4s to 6 feet.\\n   - Position the frame on the skids and toenail floor joists to the skids.\\n   - Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring to the frame.\\n   - Walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame.\\n\\n3. Passage 3:\\n   - The shed size is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\n   - Dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n   - Floor framing uses two pressure-treated 2 x 4s cut to 6 feet for rim joists.\\n\\nFrom the combination of these instructions:\\n- Site preparation includes gravel.\\n- Skids are made of 4 x 4 pressure-treated wood, typically cut to 6 feet or 70 2/4″ (which is 70.5 inches or about 5.875 feet).\\n- Floor framing uses 2 x 6 or 2 x 4 materials depending on the plan.\\n- Floor joists are attached to skids using toenailing or joist clip angles.\\n- Plywood flooring is added after framing.\\n- Walls are built separately and raised onto the frame.\\n\\nAll these steps align with standard lean-to shed construction practices.\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing a gravel base, installing skids made of pressure-treated 4 x 4 lumber, assembling a floor frame with rim joists and joists, securing the frame to the skids, attaching plywood flooring, and raising pre-built walls onto the frame.\", \"Let's analyze the passages step by step:\\n\\nStep 1: Site preparation\\n- Passage 1 mentions: \\\"Prepare the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel.\\\"\\n- This indicates that before any framing begins, the ground must be leveled and prepared with a gravel base.\\n\\nStep 2: Skid preparation\\n- Passage 1 specifies cutting two 4 x 4 skids at 70 2/4″ (which is 70.5 inches).\\n- Passage 2 states: \\\"Cut two pressure-treated 4 by 4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids.\\\"\\n- Both suggest using pressure-treated 4x4s for skids, but there is a discrepancy in length (70.5 inches vs. 6 feet). However, 6 feet equals 72 inches, so the lengths differ slightly. Given that different sheds may have different dimensions, this variation might be due to different designs.\\n\\nStep 3: Floor framing\\n- Passage 1 mentions cutting two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4 inches and six joists at 44 3/4 inches.\\n- Passage 3 refers to cutting two pressure-treated 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\\n- This suggests that floor framing can use either 2x6 or 2x4s for rim joists, depending on design and load requirements.\\n\\nStep 4: Floor assembly\\n- Passage 1 says to assemble the floor and set it on skids, then check for square and anchor with joist clip angles.\\n- Passage 2 states: \\\"Position the frame on the skids, and, when square, toenail the floor joists to the skids.\\\"\\n- Both mention ensuring the frame is square before securing it to the skids.\\n\\nStep 5: Flooring\\n- Passage 2 says: \\\"Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame.\\\"\\n- This indicates that the floor is completed with plywood after the joist and rim joist frame is installed.\\n\\nStep 6: Wall construction\\n- Passage 2 mentions: \\\"The walls for the shed are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame.\\\"\\n- This suggests that walls are built off-site and then hoisted into place.\\n\\nStep 7: Shed dimensions\\n- Passage 3 states: \\\"The shed shown here is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide. You can easily expand its dimensions in increments\", \"Step 1: Understand the task — The question asks about building a lean-to shed, and the passage provides detailed steps and materials.\\n\\nStep 2: Analyze the first passage — The first passage outlines the initial steps for building a lean-to shed. It instructs to:\\n- Prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n- Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches (which equals 70.5 inches).\\n- Set and level the skids.\\n- Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\\n- Assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids.\\n- Check for square and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles.\\n\\nStep 3: Analyze the second passage — This passage mentions:\\n- Cutting two pressure-treated 4x4s to 6 feet for skids.\\n- Positioning the frame on skids and toenailing joists to them.\\n- Attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring.\\n- Constructing walls separately and raising them onto the frame.\\n\\nStep 4: Analyze the third passage — It mentions:\\n- The shed being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\n- Dimensions can be expanded in 2 or 4-foot increments.\\n- Cutting two pressure-treated 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\\n\\nStep 5: Synthesize the information — The process of building a lean-to shed includes:\\n- Site preparation with gravel.\\n- Cutting skids and framing components (rim joists, joists).\\n- Assembling the floor frame and anchoring it to skids.\\n- Installing plywood flooring.\\n- Constructing walls separately and attaching them.\\n\\nStep 6: Determine the answer — The question is open-ended (\\\"building a lean-to shed\\\"), so provide a summary based on the passage.\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing a site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Next, cut skids (either 4x4s to 70.5 inches or 6 feet) and set them level. Cut rim joists and joists according to required dimensions, assemble the floor frame, and place it on the skids. Ensure the frame is square and anchor it using joist clip angles or toenailing. Install 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring on\"]", "time_sec": 321.896, "generation_time_seconds": 321.8931, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0005, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "126", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site, cutting and assembling the skids, flooring, and walls. To prepare the site, a 4\" layer of compacted gravel is recommended. The skids are cut to 70 2/4\" and set and leveled according to the shed building plans. Two 2 x 6 rim joists are cut at 7 3/4\" and six joists at 44 3/4\". The floor is assembled and set on the skids, checked for square, and anchored to the skids with four joist clip angles. The walls are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. Pressure-treated 4 by 4s are used for the skids, while 2 by 4s are used for the rim joists and flooring. 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood is attached to the frame for the flooring.", "pred": "- Site preparation with gravel.\n- Skids made from pressure-treated 4x4s cut to size (either 6 feet or 70 2/4 inches).\n- Floor framing with rim jo", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Analyze the given passages for information about building a lean-to shed.\n\nPassage 1:\n- Mentions preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\n- Involves cutting two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches.\n- Uses two 2x6 rim joists cut to 7 3/4 inches and six joists at 44 3/4 inches.\n- Assembles the floor frame and sets it on skids.\n- Anchors the frame to skids using joist clip angles.\n\nPassage 2:\n- Recommends cutting two pressure-treated 4x4s to 6 feet for skids.\n- Involves positioning the frame on skids and toenailing floor joists.\n- Uses 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood for flooring.\n- Walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame.\n\nPassage 3:\n- Describes a shed that is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\n- Suggests that dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\n- Specifies cutting two pressure-treated 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\n\nStep 2: Combine the relevant information across the passages.\n\n- Skids are cut from pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet (Passage 2) or 70 2/4 inches (Passage 1). Note: 70 2/4 inches equals 70.5 inches, which is approximately 5.875 feet, slightly shorter than 6 feet.\n- Floor framing includes rim joists and joists, with specific dimensions provided.\n- Floor is anchored with joist clip angles (Passage 1) or toenailed to skids (Passage 2).\n- Floor decking uses 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood (Passage 2).\n- The shed’s standard size is 6 feet by 4 feet (Passage 3), with expandability in 2- or 4-foot increments.\n\nStep 3: Determine the correct answer.\n\nThe question asks about building a lean-to shed. Based on the provided passages, a lean-to shed requires:\n\n- Site preparation with gravel.\n- Skids made from pressure-treated 4x4s cut to size (either 6 feet or 70 2/4 inches).\n- Floor framing with rim jo", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Analyze the given passages for information about building a lean-to shed.\\n\\nPassage 1:\\n- Mentions preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n- Involves cutting two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches.\\n- Uses two 2x6 rim joists cut to 7 3/4 inches and six joists at 44 3/4 inches.\\n- Assembles the floor frame and sets it on skids.\\n- Anchors the frame to skids using joist clip angles.\\n\\nPassage 2:\\n- Recommends cutting two pressure-treated 4x4s to 6 feet for skids.\\n- Involves positioning the frame on skids and toenailing floor joists.\\n- Uses 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood for flooring.\\n- Walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame.\\n\\nPassage 3:\\n- Describes a shed that is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\n- Suggests that dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n- Specifies cutting two pressure-treated 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\\n\\nStep 2: Combine the relevant information across the passages.\\n\\n- Skids are cut from pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet (Passage 2) or 70 2/4 inches (Passage 1). Note: 70 2/4 inches equals 70.5 inches, which is approximately 5.875 feet, slightly shorter than 6 feet.\\n- Floor framing includes rim joists and joists, with specific dimensions provided.\\n- Floor is anchored with joist clip angles (Passage 1) or toenailed to skids (Passage 2).\\n- Floor decking uses 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood (Passage 2).\\n- The shed’s standard size is 6 feet by 4 feet (Passage 3), with expandability in 2- or 4-foot increments.\\n\\nStep 3: Determine the correct answer.\\n\\nThe question asks about building a lean-to shed. Based on the provided passages, a lean-to shed requires:\\n\\n- Site preparation with gravel.\\n- Skids made from pressure-treated 4x4s cut to size (either 6 feet or 70 2/4 inches).\\n- Floor framing with rim jo\", \"Step 1: Understand the context. The question is about building a lean-to shed, and the provided passages offer detailed instructions and materials required for the construction process.\\n\\nStep 2: Analyze the materials and steps mentioned in the passages.\\n\\n- Passage 1 mentions preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel, cutting 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches, cutting rim joists and joists to specific lengths, assembling the floor, and anchoring it with joist clip angles.\\n- Passage 2 describes cutting 4x4s to 6 feet for skids, positioning the floor frame on the skids, toenailing floor joists, and attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring.\\n- Passage 3 states that the shed is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide and involves cutting 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\\n\\nStep 3: Understand the structural components.\\n- Skids (support beams) are cut to length and placed on a prepared site.\\n- Floor framing includes rim joists and joists that form the base of the shed.\\n- Flooring is attached to the frame.\\n- Walls are built separately and raised onto the frame.\\n\\nStep 4: Determine the overall process.\\n- Prepare the site with gravel.\\n- Install skids and ensure they are level.\\n- Assemble the floor frame with joists and rim joists.\\n- Attach floor decking (plywood).\\n- Anchor the frame to the skids.\\n- Build walls separately and raise them.\\n\\nStep 5: Synthesize the steps.\\nThe process involves site preparation, constructing the floor on skids, securing it, and later attaching the walls.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of gravel, cutting and installing skids (4x4s) to support the structure, assembling a floor frame with rim joists and joists, securing the frame to the skids using joist clip angles or toenailing, attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, and then raising pre-built walls onto the floor frame. Dimensions may vary, but the base structure is typically 6 feet by 4 feet, expandable in 2 or 4-foot increments.\", \"Step 1: Site Preparation  \\nThe first step in building a lean-to shed, as described in passage 1, is to prepare the site. This involves laying a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel to ensure a stable and level foundation.\\n\\nStep 2: Skid Preparation  \\nTwo 4 x 4 skids are cut to a specific length. According to passage 1, they should be cut to 70 2/4 inches (or 70.5 inches), while passage 2 states that pressure-treated 4 x 4s can be cut to 6 feet (72 inches). These skids serve as the base support for the shed frame.\\n\\nStep 3: Floor Framing  \\nThe floor frame construction includes cutting two 2 x 6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches in passage 1. However, passage 3 mentions cutting two pressure-treated 2 x 4s to 6 feet for rim joists. These differences may relate to variations in shed size or design, but the general idea is to frame the shed floor using joists and rim joists.\\n\\nStep 4: Assembling and Anchoring the Floor  \\nThe floor frame is assembled and placed on the skids. It should be checked for squareness and then anchored using joist clip angles or toenailed into place, as specified in passage 1 and passage 2.\\n\\nStep 5: Flooring Installation  \\nOnce the frame is secured, 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring is attached to the frame, as stated in passage 2.\\n\\nStep 6: Wall Construction  \\nWalls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. This is mentioned in passage 2.\\n\\nStep 7: Shed Size  \\nThe shed described in passage 3 is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but it can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet, allowing for flexibility in size.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing a gravel foundation, installing skids, constructing the floor frame with joists and rim joists, anchoring the frame, installing plywood flooring, and erecting walls built separately. Dimensions can be adjusted as needed.\", \"Step 1: Determine the purpose and scope of the question. The question is asking for information about building a lean-to shed, using the provided passage.\\n\\nStep 2: Extract relevant details from the passage.\\n- From Passage 1: The site preparation involves a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. The skids are made from 4 x 4s cut to 70 2/4 inches. Floor components include two 2 x 6 rim joists (7 3/4 inches) and six joists (44 3/4 inches). The floor is assembled and anchored to the skids using joist clip angles.\\n- From Passage 2: The skids are 4 by 4s cut to 6 feet. The frame is placed on the skids and secured with toenailing. Plywood flooring (3/4-inch tongue-and-groove) is attached to the frame. Walls are built separately and then raised.\\n- From Passage 3: The shed dimensions mentioned are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide. The rim joists for the floor are 2 by 4s cut to 6 feet.\\n\\nStep 3: Synthesize the information.\\n- Site preparation includes gravel.\\n- Skids are made of 4 x 4s; in one case, they are 70 2/4 inches long, while in another, they are 6 feet.\\n- Floor framing uses rim joists and joists; in one case, 2 x 6s are used, while in another, 2 x 4s are used.\\n- The floor is assembled and anchored to the skids.\\n- Flooring is added as 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood.\\n- Walls are constructed separately and raised.\\n\\nStep 4: Note inconsistencies.\\n- Skid lengths vary between 70 2/4 inches and 6 feet. This may reflect different design dimensions or errors in the passages.\\n- Use of 2 x 6 vs. 2 x 4 for rim joists may depend on design or load requirements.\\n\\nStep 5: Formulate the answer.\\nBased on the passage, building a lean-to shed involves site preparation with gravel, setting skids, assembling floor framing with joists and rim joists, anchoring the frame to the skids, attaching plywood flooring, and raising pre-built walls. Dimensions and materials may vary slightly depending on the design.\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the\"]", "time_sec": 320.499, "generation_time_seconds": 320.4959, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0004, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 20.83, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "127", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "Sure! Here's my attempt to answer your question based on the provided passages:\n\nHow do you prepare the site for building a lean-to shed?\n\nAccording to passage 1, step 1, you should prepare the site by laying down a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. This will provide a stable base for the shed's foundation. Additionally, passage 2 mentions cutting two pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids, which will serve as the foundation for the shed. Finally, passage 3 provides instructions for building the shed's floor, including cutting rim joists to a length of 6 feet. Based on these passages, it appears that preparing the site involves laying down a layer of gravel and setting the skids in place before constructing the floor.", "pred": "Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with gravel, installing skids, assembling the floor frame with rim joists and joists, anchoring it to the skids, attaching plywood flooring, and then erecting pre-built walls. The basic dimensions can be 6 feet by 4 feet, with options to expand.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Let's analyze the process of building a lean-to shed based on the provided passages:\n\n1. Site preparation:\n   - The first step mentioned in Passage 1 is to prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\n\n2. Skid installation:\n   - In Passage 1, two 4x4 skids are cut to 70 2/4 inches and set and leveled.\n   - In Passage 2, two pressure-treated 4x4s are cut to 6 feet for the skids.\n\n3. Floor frame assembly:\n   - Passage 1 mentions cutting two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\n   - The floor frame is assembled and placed on the skids, then anchored with joist clip angles.\n\n4. Floor decking:\n   - Passage 2 states that 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring is attached to the frame.\n\n5. Wall construction:\n   - Walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame, as per Passage 2.\n\n6. Shed dimensions:\n   - Passage 3 specifies that the shed is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but it also notes that dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\n\nThese steps outline the foundational and structural stages in constructing a lean-to shed.\n\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with gravel, installing skids, assembling the floor frame with rim joists and joists, anchoring it to the skids, attaching plywood flooring, and then erecting pre-built walls. The basic dimensions can be 6 feet by 4 feet, with options to expand.", "cot": "[\"Let's analyze the process of building a lean-to shed based on the provided passages:\\n\\n1. Site preparation:\\n   - The first step mentioned in Passage 1 is to prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n\\n2. Skid installation:\\n   - In Passage 1, two 4x4 skids are cut to 70 2/4 inches and set and leveled.\\n   - In Passage 2, two pressure-treated 4x4s are cut to 6 feet for the skids.\\n\\n3. Floor frame assembly:\\n   - Passage 1 mentions cutting two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\\n   - The floor frame is assembled and placed on the skids, then anchored with joist clip angles.\\n\\n4. Floor decking:\\n   - Passage 2 states that 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring is attached to the frame.\\n\\n5. Wall construction:\\n   - Walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame, as per Passage 2.\\n\\n6. Shed dimensions:\\n   - Passage 3 specifies that the shed is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but it also notes that dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n\\nThese steps outline the foundational and structural stages in constructing a lean-to shed.\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with gravel, installing skids, assembling the floor frame with rim joists and joists, anchoring it to the skids, attaching plywood flooring, and then erecting pre-built walls. The basic dimensions can be 6 feet by 4 feet, with options to expand.\", \"Step 1: Understand the context of the question. The question is asking about building a lean-to shed, which is supported by multiple passages.\\n\\nStep 2: Analyze the materials and steps involved in the construction process as described in the passages.\\n\\nPassage 1:\\n- Prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n- Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches (70.5 inches).\\n- Set and level the skids.\\n- Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\\n- Assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids.\\n- Check for squareness and anchor the frame using joist clip angles.\\n\\nPassage 2:\\n- Cut two pressure-treated 4x4s to 6 feet (72 inches) for skids.\\n- Position the floor frame on the skids.\\n- Toenail joists to skids after ensuring squareness.\\n- Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring.\\n- Walls are built separately and then raised onto the frame.\\n\\nPassage 3:\\n- The shed example is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\n- Dimensions can be expanded in 2-foot or 4-foot increments.\\n- Cut two pressure-treated 2x4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\\n\\nStep 3: Compare and integrate the instructions from all passages. While some details vary slightly (e.g., skid length differs between 70.5 inches and 6 feet), the overall process remains consistent: prepare site, set skids, assemble floor frame, secure with joist clips or toenailing, attach flooring, and then erect walls.\\n\\nStep 4: Formulate a coherent summary. The process involves site preparation, cutting and positioning skids, assembling and anchoring the floor frame, attaching flooring, and then building and raising walls.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with gravel, cutting and positioning skids, assembling the floor frame with joists and rim joists, anchoring the frame to the skids, attaching plywood flooring, and then raising the walls. Dimensions can vary, but standard sizes might include 6 feet by 4 feet, with materials such as pressure-treated lumber and tongue-and-groove plywood.\", \"Step 1: Analyze the provided passages for relevant details about building a lean-to shed.\\n\\nPassage 1:\\n- Recommends preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n- Involves cutting two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches.\\n- Requires cutting two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\\n- Floor assembly is done first and placed on skids.\\n- Anchoring the frame to skids using four joist clip angles.\\n- Emphasizes checking for squareness after assembly.\\n\\nPassage 2:\\n- Suggests cutting two pressure-treated 4x4s to 6 feet for skids.\\n- Involves positioning the frame on the skids and toenailing floor joists to skids.\\n- Uses 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood for flooring.\\n- Walls are built separately and raised onto the frame.\\n\\nPassage 3:\\n- Mentions a shed size of 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\n- Allows for expansion in 2 or 4-foot increments.\\n- Recommends using two pressure-treated 2x4s for rim joists cut to 6 feet.\\n\\nStep 2: Synthesize the information.\\n\\n- Site preparation includes a gravel base (4 inches).\\n- Skids are typically made of 4x4s and either cut to specific lengths (70 2/4 inches or 6 feet) depending on the plan.\\n- Floor framing uses 2x6 or 2x4 rim joists and joists of varying lengths.\\n- Floor is assembled on a frame and then placed on skids.\\n- Anchoring with joist clip angles or toenailing is used.\\n- Materials like tongue-and-groove plywood are used for flooring.\\n- Walls are built separately and later raised onto the framed base.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nAll steps align with constructing a lean-to shed: site preparation, skid installation, floor framing, anchoring, flooring, and wall assembly.\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing a gravel base, installing skids (typically 4x4s), assembling a floor frame using rim joists and joists, anchoring the frame to skids, attaching plywood flooring, and later raising walls built separately.\", \"Step 1: Understand the task — The question is asking about building a lean-to shed, and we need to use the provided passages to answer it.\\n\\nStep 2: Identify relevant information from the passages.\\n\\nFrom passage 1:\\n- Prepare the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel.\\n- Cut two 4 x 4 skids at 70 2/4″.\\n- Set and level the skids.\\n- Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4″ and six joists at 44 3/4″.\\n- Assemble the floor and set it on the skids.\\n- Check for square, anchor the frame to the skids with joist clip angles.\\n\\nFrom passage 2:\\n- Cut two pressure-treated 4 by 4s to 6 feet for skids.\\n- Position the frame on the skids.\\n- Toenail floor joists to skids.\\n- Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring.\\n- Build walls separately and raise them onto the frame.\\n\\nFrom passage 3:\\n- The shed is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\n- Can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n- Cut two pressure-treated 2 by 4s to 6 feet for rim joists.\\n\\nStep 3: Synthesize the information into a coherent answer.\\n\\nTo build a lean-to shed:\\n1. Prepare the site by laying a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n2. Cut skids (either 4 x 4s or pressure-treated 4x4s) to the required length (e.g., 6 feet or 70 2/4″ depending on the plan).\\n3. Set and level the skids.\\n4. Cut rim joists and floor joists to appropriate lengths (e.g., 2 x 6 or 2 x 4s).\\n5. Assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids.\\n6. Ensure the frame is square and anchor it to the skids using joist clip angles or toenail the joists.\\n7. Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring to the frame.\\n8. Build walls separately, then raise them onto the floor frame.\\n\\nThe shed dimensions can be customized, starting from 6 feet by 4 feet, and expanded as needed.\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing a gravel base, installing skids\"]", "time_sec": 302.881, "generation_time_seconds": 302.8781, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0006, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 26.89, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "129", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "What are the dimensions of the shed in passage 3?\n\nThe dimensions of the shed in passage 3 are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "pred": "Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site, cutting and assembling floor components, anchoring the frame, attaching the flooring, and then erecting the walls.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Prepare the site by laying a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\nStep 2: Cut two 4x4 skids to the required length (70 2/4 inches according to passage 1).\nStep 3: Set and level the skids on the prepared site following the shed building plans.\nStep 4: Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\nStep 5: Assemble the floor frame using the rim joists and joists.\nStep 6: Place the assembled floor frame onto the skids.\nStep 7: Check the frame for squareness and anchor it to the skids using joist clip angles.\nStep 8: For the floor, attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood over the frame.\nStep 9: Build the walls separately, then raise and attach them to the floor frame.\nStep 10: The shed dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\n\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site, cutting and assembling floor components, anchoring the frame, attaching the flooring, and then erecting the walls.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Prepare the site by laying a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\nStep 2: Cut two 4x4 skids to the required length (70 2/4 inches according to passage 1).\\nStep 3: Set and level the skids on the prepared site following the shed building plans.\\nStep 4: Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\\nStep 5: Assemble the floor frame using the rim joists and joists.\\nStep 6: Place the assembled floor frame onto the skids.\\nStep 7: Check the frame for squareness and anchor it to the skids using joist clip angles.\\nStep 8: For the floor, attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood over the frame.\\nStep 9: Build the walls separately, then raise and attach them to the floor frame.\\nStep 10: The shed dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site, cutting and assembling floor components, anchoring the frame, attaching the flooring, and then erecting the walls.\", \"To build a lean-to shed, follow these steps based on the provided passages:\\n\\n1. Prepare the site: Lay down a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel to ensure a stable and level base for the shed.\\n\\n2. Cut and set skids: Cut two 4x4 skids to a length of 70 2/4 inches (as per passage 1) or 6 feet (as mentioned in passage 2). Position the skids and level them according to the shed building plans.\\n\\n3. Construct the floor frame: Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six floor joists to 44 3/4 inches (passage 1). Alternatively, you can use pressure-treated 2x4s cut to 6 feet for the rim joists (passage 3). Assemble the floor frame.\\n\\n4. Set and secure the floor: Place the assembled floor frame onto the skids. Ensure it is square, then anchor it using joist clip angles (passage 1) or toenail the floor joists to the skids (passage 2).\\n\\n5. Add flooring: Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the floor frame (passage 2).\\n\\n6. Build and raise walls: Construct the walls separately, then raise them onto the floor frame.\\n\\nThe shed dimensions can vary, with the example given as 6 feet long by 4 feet wide and expandable in increments of 2 or 4 feet (passage 3).\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing a gravel base, setting skids, assembling and anchoring a floor frame, laying tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, and raising pre-built walls. Dimensions are customizable, commonly starting at 6 feet by 4 feet.\", \"Step 1: Analyze the information about the size of the shed.\\nFrom passage 3, we learn that \\\"The shed shown here is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\\" This gives us the dimensions of the specific lean-to shed being discussed.\\n\\nStep 2: Consider the possibility of expanding dimensions.\\nPassage 3 also states, \\\"You can easily expand its dimensions in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\\" This means that while the example provided is 6 x 4 feet, the design allows for modifications to larger sizes.\\n\\nStep 3: Evaluate the framing materials mentioned.\\nPassage 1 mentions cutting 4 x 4 skids and 2 x 6 rim joists and joists. Passage 2 specifies pressure-treated 4 by 4s for skids and 2 by 4s for rim joists. There is some variation in materials, but both describe similar foundational elements.\\n\\nStep 4: Determine the key steps in building the shed.\\nPassage 1 outlines preparing the site with gravel, cutting skids and joists, assembling the floor frame, and anchoring it to the skids. Passage 2 adds toenailing joists, attaching tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, and raising walls that were built separately.\\n\\nStep 5: Note that the construction method involves a floor frame placed on skids, with walls built separately and then raised.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with compacted gravel, cutting skids and joists, assembling the floor frame, anchoring it to the skids, attaching plywood flooring, and raising pre-built walls onto the frame. The base size is 6 feet by 4 feet, but dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\", \"Step 1: Analyze the instructions for building a lean-to shed in passage 1.\\n- Prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n- Cut two 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4 inches.\\n- Set and level the skids.\\n- Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches.\\n- Assemble the floor and set it on the skids.\\n- Check for squareness.\\n- Anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles.\\n\\nStep 2: Review the second passage for additional relevant details.\\n- Cut two pressure-treated 4 x 4s to 6 feet for the skids.\\n- Position the floor frame on the skids.\\n- Toenail the floor joists to the skids once the frame is square.\\n- Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame.\\n- Walls are built separately and raised onto the frame.\\n\\nStep 3: Consider the third passage for dimensions.\\n- The shed is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\n- Dimensions can be expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n\\nStep 4: Synthesize the information.\\n- Site preparation: Use a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n- Skids: Cut 4 x 4 pressure-treated posts to 6 feet or 70 2/4 inches depending on the plan.\\n- Floor frame: Use 2 x 6 rim joists and 2 x 4 joists with proper dimensions.\\n- Assemble floor, place on skids, check for square, and anchor with joist clip angles or toenailing.\\n- Add plywood flooring.\\n- Build walls separately and raise them onto the frame.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with compacted gravel, cutting skids and framing materials to specific dimensions, assembling the floor on skids, anchoring it securely, adding plywood flooring, and raising pre-built walls onto the frame.\"]", "time_sec": 236.817, "generation_time_seconds": 236.8121, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 6.67, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "130", "question": "difference between vietnam and india", "gt": "The passages suggest that trends in India are more typical than those in Vietnam, particularly in the area of education. Vietnam's education system is described as relatively equitable, where poorer children receive the same treatment as their better-off counterparts. In contrast, India's early focus was on getting children into school, which involved setting up schools, and training teachers. Politically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War. Both countries have a history of colonialism, with India being a former colony of the United Kingdom and Vietnam of France. Post-colonization, India experienced division and conflict among Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs. Cultural and economic links between India and Vietnam date back to the 2nd century.", "pred": "Vietnam has a more equitable education system where poorer children receive the same opportunities as wealthier ones, while India's education trends are more typical of global patterns with potential disparities. Historically, India opposed colonialism and supported Vietnam’s independence, but India itself was partitioned after independence in 1947, while Vietnam experienced a civil war and conflict between North and South. Additionally, India and Vietnam have had long-standing cultural and economic ties dating back to the 2nd century.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "The passage provides several key differences between Vietnam and India:\n\n1. **Education Trends**: The passage states that \"the trends in India are much more typical than are the trends in Vietnam.\" This suggests that India's educational developments follow broader global patterns, whereas Vietnam's situation is less typical.\n\n2. **Equity in Education**: Vietnam's education system is described as \"relatively equitable,\" meaning poorer children receive the same educational opportunities as wealthier children. In contrast, this level of equity may not be as evident in India, as the passage implies a more typical pattern of disparity.\n\n3. **Historical Relations**: India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War. This shows a historical diplomatic support, even though India did not engage in military action.\n\n4. **Colonial History**: The United Kingdom lost India in 1947, and France lost Indochina (modern-day Vietnam) in 1945. Both colonies were economically significant for their colonizers, but India's decolonization resulted in the partition of the country into Hindu-majority India and Muslim-majority Pakistan (later Bangladesh), which led to significant conflicts. Vietnam, on the other hand, experienced a civil war between North and South after French withdrawal, leading to the Vietnam War.\n\n5. **Cultural and Economic Links**: India and Vietnam have had cultural and economic connections since the 2nd century, with the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influencing Vietnamese music, indicating a deep historical interaction.\n\nIn summary, the key differences lie in education equity (Vietnam being more equitable), historical decolonization outcomes (India’s partition versus Vietnam’s civil conflict), and India’s diplomatic support for Vietnam during its independence and war periods.\n\nAnswer: Vietnam has a more equitable education system where poorer children receive the same opportunities as wealthier ones, while India's education trends are more typical of global patterns with potential disparities. Historically, India opposed colonialism and supported Vietnam’s independence, but India itself was partitioned after independence in 1947, while Vietnam experienced a civil war and conflict between North and South. Additionally, India and Vietnam have had long-standing cultural and economic ties dating back to the 2nd century.", "cot": "[\"The passage provides several key differences between Vietnam and India:\\n\\n1. **Education Trends**: The passage states that \\\"the trends in India are much more typical than are the trends in Vietnam.\\\" This suggests that India's educational developments follow broader global patterns, whereas Vietnam's situation is less typical.\\n\\n2. **Equity in Education**: Vietnam's education system is described as \\\"relatively equitable,\\\" meaning poorer children receive the same educational opportunities as wealthier children. In contrast, this level of equity may not be as evident in India, as the passage implies a more typical pattern of disparity.\\n\\n3. **Historical Relations**: India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War. This shows a historical diplomatic support, even though India did not engage in military action.\\n\\n4. **Colonial History**: The United Kingdom lost India in 1947, and France lost Indochina (modern-day Vietnam) in 1945. Both colonies were economically significant for their colonizers, but India's decolonization resulted in the partition of the country into Hindu-majority India and Muslim-majority Pakistan (later Bangladesh), which led to significant conflicts. Vietnam, on the other hand, experienced a civil war between North and South after French withdrawal, leading to the Vietnam War.\\n\\n5. **Cultural and Economic Links**: India and Vietnam have had cultural and economic connections since the 2nd century, with the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influencing Vietnamese music, indicating a deep historical interaction.\\n\\nIn summary, the key differences lie in education equity (Vietnam being more equitable), historical decolonization outcomes (India’s partition versus Vietnam’s civil conflict), and India’s diplomatic support for Vietnam during its independence and war periods.\\n\\nAnswer: Vietnam has a more equitable education system where poorer children receive the same opportunities as wealthier ones, while India's education trends are more typical of global patterns with potential disparities. Historically, India opposed colonialism and supported Vietnam’s independence, but India itself was partitioned after independence in 1947, while Vietnam experienced a civil war and conflict between North and South. Additionally, India and Vietnam have had long-standing cultural and economic ties dating back to the 2nd century.\", \"Step 1: Analyze the information about India and Vietnam in the passages.\\n\\nFrom Passage 1:\\n- India's education trends are described as \\\"much more typical\\\" compared to Vietnam.\\n- In India, the early focus was on getting children into school, involving resources, facilities, building schools, and training teachers.\\n- Vietnam's education system is described as relatively equitable, meaning poorer children receive the same opportunities as better-off children.\\n\\nFrom Passage 2:\\n- India supported Vietnam's independence from France.\\n- India opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War.\\n- India supported the unification of Vietnam.\\n- India and Vietnam have strong bilateral relations.\\n- Cultural and economic ties between India and Vietnam date back to the 2nd century.\\n- The Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influenced Vietnamese music.\\n\\nFrom Passage 3:\\n- The United Kingdom lost India in 1947.\\n- France lost its colony of Indochina (modern-day Vietnam) in 1945.\\n- Both colonies were economically significant to their colonizers.\\n- After decolonization, India experienced division between Hindus, Muslims, and Sikhs, leading to conflicts.\\n- Both countries were colonized by European powers but lost their colonies at different times.\\n\\nStep 2: Identify key differences between India and Vietnam.\\n\\n- India was colonized by the British, while Vietnam was colonized by the French.\\n- India gained independence in 1947, whereas Vietnam gained independence from France in 1945.\\n- After independence, India experienced religious and political division, leading to conflict; no such conflict is mentioned for Vietnam in the passage.\\n- Vietnam has a more equitable education system compared to India, where early efforts focused on access to schools.\\n\\nStep 3: Conclude and prepare the final answer.\\n\\nThe differences between India and Vietnam include their colonial history (British vs. French), the timing of independence (India in 1947, Vietnam in 1945), post-independence conditions (India experienced religious division and conflict, while Vietnam's education system is more equitable), and historical support from India to Vietnam during its war for independence.\\n\\nAnswer: India and Vietnam differ in their colonial history, with India being colonized by the British and Vietnam by the French. India gained independence in 1947, while Vietnam gained independence from France in 1945. After independence, India experienced religious division and conflicts, unlike Vietnam, where the education system is relatively equitable, offering similar opportunities to poorer and wealthier children. Additionally\", \"Step 1: Analyze the provided passages for differences between Vietnam and India.\\n\\nFrom passage 1:\\n- The education trends in India are described as \\\"much more typical\\\" than those in Vietnam.\\n- Vietnam's education system is relatively equitable, meaning poorer children receive the same educational opportunities as wealthier children.\\n- India's early focus in education was on getting children into school, which involved setting up schools, training teachers, and allocating resources.\\n\\nFrom passage 2:\\n- India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War.\\n- India supported North Vietnam during the war.\\n- India and Vietnam have strong bilateral relations and historical cultural and economic ties dating back to the 2nd century.\\n- The Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influenced Vietnamese music.\\n\\nFrom passage 3:\\n- The United Kingdom lost India in 1947 after colonial rule.\\n- France lost its colony of Indochina (modern-day Vietnam) in 1945 after World War II.\\n- India became divided along religious lines (Hindus, Muslims, Sikhs) after independence, leading to conflict.\\n- Vietnam, after decolonization, did not face religious division like India.\\n\\nStep 2: Identify key differences based on these points.\\n\\n1. Colonial history and decolonization:\\n- India gained independence from British colonial rule in 1947.\\n- Vietnam gained independence from French colonial rule in 1945.\\n\\n2. Post-independence outcomes:\\n- India experienced religious division and conflict after independence.\\n- Vietnam did not face religious divisions like India.\\n\\n3. Education systems:\\n- Vietnam’s education system is described as more equitable.\\n- India’s early education focus was on access (getting children into schools).\\n\\n4. Historical relations:\\n- India supported Vietnam’s independence and during its war.\\n- India and Vietnam have longstanding cultural and economic ties.\\n\\nStep 3: Formulate a clear answer based on these points.\\n\\nAnswer: Vietnam and India differ in colonial history, post-independence outcomes, and education systems. India gained independence from Britain in 1947 and experienced religious divisions leading to conflict, while Vietnam gained independence from France in 1945 without such religious divisions. Vietnam has a more equitable education system, whereas India's early focus was on expanding school access. Additionally, India supported Vietnam's independence and has long-standing cultural and diplomatic ties with Vietnam.\", \"Step 1: Consider the education systems in Vietnam and India.\\n- The passage mentions that India's education trends are \\\"much more typical\\\" than Vietnam's.\\n- Vietnam's education system is described as \\\"relatively equitable,\\\" meaning poorer children receive the same educational opportunities as wealthier ones.\\n- In India, the early focus was on getting children into school, which involved setting up schools, training teachers, etc.\\n\\nStep 2: Examine historical relations and independence movements.\\n- India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War.\\n- India and Vietnam share a history of cultural and economic links dating back to the 2nd century, with the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influencing Vietnamese music.\\n\\nStep 3: Analyze colonial history.\\n- The United Kingdom lost India in 1947.\\n- France lost its colony of Indochina (modern-day Vietnam) in 1945.\\n- Both were large colonies and economically significant for their colonizers.\\n\\nStep 4: Consider current bilateral relations.\\n- India and Vietnam have strong bilateral relations.\\n\\nStep 5: Determine differences based on the information provided.\\n- Vietnam has an equitable education system where socioeconomic background does not significantly affect access to education.\\n- India’s education system historically focused on access, with efforts to expand infrastructure and enrollment.\\n- Vietnam was colonized by France, while India was colonized by the United Kingdom.\\n- After independence, India experienced religious partition and conflict, while the passage does not mention similar outcomes for Vietnam.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Vietnam and India differ in several ways. Vietnam’s education system is relatively equitable, providing equal opportunities regardless of socioeconomic background, whereas India focused historically on expanding access to education. Vietnam was colonized by France and gained independence in 1945, while India was colonized by the United Kingdom and gained independence in 1947. After independence, India experienced partition between Hindus, Muslims, and Sikhs, leading to conflicts, while Vietnam’s post-independence situation is not described as similarly conflicted. Additionally, India and Vietnam have strong bilateral relations and shared historical cultural links.\"]", "time_sec": 306.036, "generation_time_seconds": 306.0308, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.48, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "132", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "Preheat your oven to 400°F (205° C). Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the skins of the individual cloves intact. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to a 1/2 inch from the top of cloves to expose them. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves. Season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices if you like. Roasting time is dependent on the size of the cloves and may take up to 45 minutes or an hour for larger cloves. Once roasted, the garlic cloves can be stored in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.", "pred": "To roast individual garlic cloves, prepare whole garlic heads by peeling the outer layers, cutting the top off to expose the cloves, placing them cut side up in a pan, drizzling with olive oil, and roasting at 400°F until soft. Seasonings can be added before roasting.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Start with whole heads of garlic. Peel and discard the outer papery layers, but keep the individual cloves intact with their skins on.\n\nStep 2: Using a sharp knife, cut about 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the garlic head to expose the individual cloves.\n\nStep 3: Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan can help keep them stable.\n\nStep 4: Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each garlic head, and rub the oil over the exposed cloves using your fingers.\n\nStep 5: Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C). Roast the garlic until the cloves are soft, which may take up to 45 minutes or even an hour depending on the size of the cloves.\n\nStep 6: Optionally, season the garlic with herbs or spices such as cayenne, chipotle, Cajun seasoning, curry powder, or dried herbs like oregano, thyme, or rosemary to complement your dish.\n\nAnswer: To roast individual garlic cloves, prepare whole garlic heads by peeling the outer layers, cutting the top off to expose the cloves, placing them cut side up in a pan, drizzling with olive oil, and roasting at 400°F until soft. Seasonings can be added before roasting.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Start with whole heads of garlic. Peel and discard the outer papery layers, but keep the individual cloves intact with their skins on.\\n\\nStep 2: Using a sharp knife, cut about 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the garlic head to expose the individual cloves.\\n\\nStep 3: Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan can help keep them stable.\\n\\nStep 4: Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each garlic head, and rub the oil over the exposed cloves using your fingers.\\n\\nStep 5: Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C). Roast the garlic until the cloves are soft, which may take up to 45 minutes or even an hour depending on the size of the cloves.\\n\\nStep 6: Optionally, season the garlic with herbs or spices such as cayenne, chipotle, Cajun seasoning, curry powder, or dried herbs like oregano, thyme, or rosemary to complement your dish.\\n\\nAnswer: To roast individual garlic cloves, prepare whole garlic heads by peeling the outer layers, cutting the top off to expose the cloves, placing them cut side up in a pan, drizzling with olive oil, and roasting at 400°F until soft. Seasonings can be added before roasting.\", \"To roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\\n2. Prepare the garlic by peeling and discarding the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, while keeping the individual cloves intact.\\n3. Use a sharp knife to cut about 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual garlic cloves.\\n4. Place the prepared garlic heads into a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan is ideal as it prevents the garlic bulbs from rolling.\\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each garlic head, and use your fingers to rub the olive oil evenly over the exposed cloves.\\n6. Roast in the preheated oven. The roasting time depends on the size of the cloves—small cloves may cook faster, while large cloves may require up to 45 minutes to an hour until softened.\\n7. Optionally, season the garlic with herbs and spices such as cayenne, chipotle pepper powder, Cajun seasoning, curry powder, oregano, thyme, rosemary, tarragon, or basil, depending on the dish you plan to serve it with.\\n8. Once roasted, store any leftover cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\\n\\nAnswer: To roast individual garlic cloves, preheat the oven to 400°F, cut the top off the garlic head to expose cloves, drizzle with olive oil, and roast until soft, which may take up to an hour depending on size. Seasoning can be added before roasting.\", \"Step 1: Select one or more whole heads of garlic.\\nStep 2: Preheat the oven to 400°F (205°C).\\nStep 3: Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the individual cloves intact within their skins.\\nStep 4: Use a sharp knife to cut off about 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual garlic cloves.\\nStep 5: Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan is helpful to keep them from rolling.\\nStep 6: Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head and use your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\\nStep 7: Cook in the preheated oven until the cloves are soft — this may take up to 45 minutes to an hour depending on the size of the cloves.\\nStep 8: Optional: Season after roasting with herbs and spices such as cayenne, chipotle pepper powder, Cajun seasoning, curry powder, oregano, thyme, rosemary, or basil, depending on your preference.\\n\\nAnswer: To roast individual garlic cloves, preheat the oven to 400°F (205°C), peel the outer layers from a whole garlic bulb while keeping the cloves intact, cut the top 1/4 to 1/2 inch off the cloves to expose them, place the bulb in a baking pan cut side up, drizzle with olive oil, and roast until soft—up to 45 minutes to an hour depending on clove size.\", \"Step 1: Start with whole heads of garlic, not individual cloves. This is key because roasting whole heads helps maintain the structure of the cloves during cooking.\\n\\nStep 2: Preheat the oven to 400°F (205°C).\\n\\nStep 3: Peel the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the individual cloves intact with their skins still on.\\n\\nStep 4: Use a sharp knife to cut about 1/4 to 1/2 inch off the top of the cloves, exposing the individual garlic cloves inside.\\n\\nStep 5: Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan can be useful to keep them from rolling.\\n\\nStep 6: Drizzle olive oil (about two teaspoons per head) over the exposed cloves and rub it in with your fingers.\\n\\nStep 7: Roast in the oven for 30 to 45 minutes, depending on the size of the cloves. Larger cloves may require up to 60 minutes to soften.\\n\\nStep 8: Once roasted, remove from oven and allow to cool slightly. The cloves can be squeezed out of their skins and used as needed.\\n\\nAnswer: To roast individual garlic cloves, use whole heads of garlic. Cut the top off the head to expose the cloves, drizzle with olive oil, and roast at 400°F for 30–60 minutes depending on size.\"]", "time_sec": 210.721, "generation_time_seconds": 210.718, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0004, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.38, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
