ystem, sending cool water back to the heater via a return line to be reheated. This circulation can be controlled manually (e.g., by pressing a button) or automatically (e.g., using a timer or motion sensor).\", \"Step 1: Understand the context of the question.  \\nThe question is asking how to make a hot water heater circulate. This refers to creating a circulation system where hot water continuously moves through the pipes to ensure that hot water is available immediately when a faucet is turned on.\\n\\nStep 2: Refer to passage 2.  \\nPassage 2 explains that a hot water circulation pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. This pump circulates water continuously through the water heater, ensuring that hot water is always available.\\n\\nStep 3: Refer to passage 3.  \\nPassage 3 mentions that recirculating pumps rapidly pull hot water from the water heater and simultaneously send cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused. It also notes that demand systems can be controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor.\\n\\nStep 4: Synthesize the information.  \\nTo make a hot water heater circulate, a recirculating pump is used. The pump pulls hot water from the heater and sends it through the pipes while simultaneously returning cooler water from the pipes back to the heater to be reheated. This creates a continuous loop. The system can be automatic (always on) or demand-based, controlled by a button, timer, or sensor.\\n\\nFinal Answer: To make a hot water heater circulate, install a recirculating pump that pulls hot water from the heater and sends it through the pipes while returning cooler water back to the heater to be reheated, forming a continuous loop. This can be done automatically or controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor.\"]", "time_sec": 189.293, "generation_time_seconds": 189.2913, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0002, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.78, "GAVIE-Rel": -1